-- V473: Seed 122 Class 11 Chemistry Mock Tests (104 Real-Format 70M Papers + 18 Chapter-Wise 25M Mocks)

DO $$
DECLARE
    v_class_id UUID;
BEGIN
    SELECT id INTO v_class_id FROM learn_exam_types WHERE slug IN ('class-11', 'cbse-class-11') LIMIT 1;
    IF v_class_id IS NULL THEN
        SELECT id INTO v_class_id FROM learn_exam_types WHERE name ILIKE '%Class 11%' LIMIT 1;
    END IF;

    IF v_class_id IS NOT NULL THEN
        DELETE FROM learn_test_questions WHERE mock_test_id IN (SELECT id FROM learn_mock_tests WHERE id::text LIKE '11020000-%');
        DELETE FROM learn_mock_tests WHERE id::text LIKE '11020000-%';

        ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

Reason (R): Density of water is 1 g/mL, so 18 mL equals 18 g = 1 molar mass of H₂O.
Choose the correct option:
A) Both A and R are true, and R is the correct explanation of A.
B) Both A and R are true, and R is not the correct explanation of A.
C) A is true but R is false.
D) A is false but R is true.', '{"A":"Both A and R are true, and R is the correct explanation of A","B":"Both A and R are true, and R is not the correct explanation of A","C":"A is true but R is false","D":"A is false but R is true"}'::jsonb, 'A', 'ASSERTION_REASON', 'Chemistry', 'Some Basic Concepts of Chemistry', 1, 'Mass = 18 g => 1 mole.', NULL, NULL, 'Mass = 18 g => 1 mole.', 'EASY', true),
        ('11021111-0000-4000-8000-000000000007', 'Define Limiting Reagent and explain its role in stoichiometric yield calculations.', NULL, NULL, 'VSA', 'Chemistry', 'Some Basic Concepts of Chemistry', 2, 'Reactant completely consumed first in a chemical reaction, limiting the maximum amount of product formed.', 'Definition (1M) + Stoichiometric role (1M)', NULL, 'Reactant completely consumed first in a chemical reaction, limiting the maximum amount of product formed.', 'MEDIUM', true),
        ('11021111-0000-4000-8000-000000000008', 'Calculate molarity of NaOH in a solution prepared by dissolving 4 g NaOH in enough water to form 250 mL solution.', NULL, NULL, 'VSA', 'Chemistry', 'Some Basic Concepts of Chemistry', 2, 'Moles = 4/40 = 0.1 mol. V = 0.25 L. M = 0.1/0.25 = 0.4 M.', 'Moles (1M) + 0.4 M (1M)', NULL, 'Moles = 4/40 = 0.1 mol. V = 0.25 L. M = 0.1/0.25 = 0.4 M.', 'MEDIUM', true),
        ('11021111-0000-4000-8000-000000000009', 'A compound contains 4.07% hydrogen, 24.27% carbon, and 71.65% chlorine. Its molar mass is 98.96 g/mol. Find its empirical and molecular formulae.', NULL, NULL, 'SA', 'Chemistry', 'Some Basic Concepts of Chemistry', 3, 'C: 24.27/12=2.02; H: 4.07/1=4.07; Cl: 71.65/35.5=2.02 -> Ratio 1:2:1 => Empirical = CH₂Cl (mass 49.5). n = 98.96/49.5 = 2 -> Molecular = C₂H₄Cl₂.', 'Mole ratios (1.5M) + Empirical CH₂Cl (0.5M) + Molecular C₂H₄Cl₂ (1M)', NULL, 'C: 24.27/12=2.02; H: 4.07/1=4.07; Cl: 71.65/35.5=2.02 -> Ratio 1:2:1 => Empirical = CH₂Cl (mass 49.5). n = 98.96/49.5 = 2 -> Molecular = C₂H₄Cl₂.', 'MEDIUM', true),
        ('11021111-0000-4000-8000-00000000000a', '50.0 kg of N₂(g) and 10.0 kg of H₂(g) are mixed to produce NH₃(g). Calculate the amount of NH₃ formed and identify the limiting reagent.', NULL, NULL, 'SA', 'Chemistry', 'Some Basic Concepts of Chemistry', 3, 'N₂: 50000/28 = 1785.7 mol; H₂: 10000/2 = 5000 mol. Need 3×1785.7 = 5357 mol H₂ (>5000) => H₂ is limiting. NH₃ = (2/3)×5000 = 3333.3 mol = 56.67 kg.', 'Moles & limiting H₂ (1.5M) + 56.67 kg NH₃ (1.5M)', NULL, 'N₂: 50000/28 = 1785.7 mol; H₂: 10000/2 = 5000 mol. Need 3×1785.7 = 5357 mol H₂ (>5000) => H₂ is limiting. NH₃ = (2/3)×5000 = 3333.3 mol = 56.67 kg.', 'MEDIUM', true),
        ('11021111-0000-4000-8000-00000000000b', 'State all four Laws of Chemical Combination (Conservation of Mass, Definite Proportions, Multiple Proportions, Avogadro''s Law). Density of 3 M solution of NaCl is 1.25 g/mL. Calculate molality of the solution.', NULL, NULL, 'LA', 'Chemistry', 'Some Basic Concepts of Chemistry', 5, 'Four laws stated clearly with examples (3M). 1 L solution contains 3 mol NaCl = 3×58.5 = 175.5 g NaCl. Mass of 1 L solution = 1250 g -> Water mass = 1250 - 175.5 = 1074.5 g = 1.0745 kg. Molality m = 3 / 1.0745 = 2.79 m (2M).', 'Laws (3M) + Molality 2.79 m (2M)', NULL, 'Four laws stated clearly with examples (3M). 1 L solution contains 3 mol NaCl = 3×58.5 = 175.5 g NaCl. Mass of 1 L solution = 1250 g -> Water mass = 1250 - 175.5 = 1074.5 g = 1.0745 kg. Molality m = 3 / 1.0745 = 2.79 m (2M).', 'HARD', true),
        ('11021111-0000-4000-8000-00000000000c', '(i) Formula connecting Molarity M₁, V₁ and M₂, V₂ upon dilution. (ii) Volume of 2 M HCl needed to prepare 500 mL of 0.5 M HCl. (iii) Mole fraction sum of all components in a mixture. (iv) Convert 20% (w/w) aqueous KI into molality if M_KI = 166 g/mol.', NULL, NULL, 'CASE', 'Chemistry', 'Some Basic Concepts of Chemistry', 4, '(i) M₁V₁ = M₂V₂. (ii) 2 × V₁ = 0.5 × 500 => V₁ = 125 mL. (iii) Always equal to 1. (iv) 20 g KI in 80 g H₂O -> m = (20/166)/0.080 = 1.51 m.', '4 × 1M = 4M', 'A student prepares standard acid solutions in a volumetric flask by diluting 2 M stock HCl and verifying concentration units resistant to ambient temperature changes.', '(i) M₁V₁ = M₂V₂. (ii) 2 × V₁ = 0.5 × 500 => V₁ = 125 mL. (iii) Always equal to 1. (iv) 20 g KI in 80 g H₂O -> m = (20/166)/0.080 = 1.51 m.', 'HARD', true),
        ('11021111-0000-4000-8000-00000000000d', 'Maximum number of electrons in a shell with principal quantum number n is:', '{"A":"n²","B":"2n²","C":"2l + 1","D":"4l + 2"}'::jsonb, 'B', 'MCQ', 'Chemistry', 'Structure of Atom', 1, '2n² electrons across n² orbitals.', NULL, NULL, '2n² electrons across n² orbitals.', 'EASY', true),
        ('11021111-0000-4000-8000-00000000000e', 'Which set of quantum numbers (n, l, m_l, m_s) is NOT permissible?', '{"A":"(3, 2, -2, +1/2)","B":"(3, 3, 0, -1/2)","C":"(2, 1, 0, +1/2)","D":"(4, 0, 0, -1/2)"}'::jsonb, 'B', 'MCQ', 'Chemistry', 'Structure of Atom', 1, 'l must be 0 to n-1; for n=3, l=3 is forbidden.', NULL, NULL, 'l must be 0 to n-1; for n=3, l=3 is forbidden.', 'EASY', true),
        ('11021111-0000-4000-8000-00000000000f', 'Number of radial nodes and angular nodes in a 4p orbital respectively are:', '{"A":"2 and 1","B":"1 and 2","C":"3 and 1","D":"2 and 2"}'::jsonb, 'A', 'MCQ', 'Chemistry', 'Structure of Atom', 1, 'Radial = n-l-1 = 4-1-1 = 2; Angular = l = 1.', NULL, NULL, 'Radial = n-l-1 = 4-1-1 = 2; Angular = l = 1.', 'EASY', true),
        ('11021111-0000-4000-8000-000000000010', 'de Broglie wavelength λ of a particle of momentum p is:', '{"A":"h × p","B":"h / p","C":"p / h","D":"h / p²"}'::jsonb, 'B', 'MCQ', 'Chemistry', 'Structure of Atom', 1, 'λ = h / (mv) = h / p.', NULL, NULL, 'λ = h / (mv) = h / p.', 'EASY', true),
        ('11021111-0000-4000-8000-000000000011', 'Which transition in a hydrogen atom emits a photon in the visible Balmer series?', '{"A":"n=4 -> n=1","B":"n=4 -> n=2","C":"n=5 -> n=3","D":"n=2 -> n=1"}'::jsonb, 'B', 'MCQ', 'Chemistry', 'Structure of Atom', 1, 'Transitions ending at n₁ = 2 form Balmer visible lines.', NULL, NULL, 'Transitions ending at n₁ = 2 form Balmer visible lines.', 'EASY', true),
        ('11021111-0000-4000-8000-000000000012', 'Assertion (A): Half-filled (d⁵) and completely filled (d¹⁰) subshells impart extra stability, explaining Chromium (Cr) configuration [Ar] 3d⁵ 4s¹.
Reason (R): Symmetrical distribution of electrons and maximum exchange energy stabilise degenerate half-filled orbitals.
Choose the correct option:
A) Both A and R are true, and R is the correct explanation of A.
B) Both A and R are true, and R is not the correct explanation of A.
C) A is true but R is false.
D) A is false but R is true.', '{"A":"Both A and R are true, and R is the correct explanation of A","B":"Both A and R are true, and R is not the correct explanation of A","C":"A is true but R is false","D":"A is false but R is true"}'::jsonb, 'A', 'ASSERTION_REASON', 'Chemistry', 'Structure of Atom', 1, 'Exchange energy + symmetry stabilise 3d⁵ 4s¹.', NULL, NULL, 'Exchange energy + symmetry stabilise 3d⁵ 4s¹.', 'EASY', true),
        ('11021111-0000-4000-8000-000000000013', 'State Heisenberg''s Uncertainty Principle and write its mathematical inequality.', NULL, NULL, 'VSA', 'Chemistry', 'Structure of Atom', 2, 'It is impossible to determine simultaneously the exact position and exact momentum of a microscopic moving particle: Δx · Δp ≥ h / (4π).', 'Statement (1M) + Inequality (1M)', NULL, 'It is impossible to determine simultaneously the exact position and exact momentum of a microscopic moving particle: Δx · Δp ≥ h / (4π).', 'MEDIUM', true),
        ('11021111-0000-4000-8000-000000000014', 'Why is Pauli''s Exclusion Principle essential in filling atomic orbitals? State Hund''s Rule of Maximum Multiplicity.', NULL, NULL, 'VSA', 'Chemistry', 'Structure of Atom', 2, 'Pauli restricts each orbital to maximum 2 electrons of opposite spin. Hund states pairing in degenerate orbitals starts only after every orbital receives one parallel-spin electron.', '1M each', NULL, 'Pauli restricts each orbital to maximum 2 electrons of opposite spin. Hund states pairing in degenerate orbitals starts only after every orbital receives one parallel-spin electron.', 'MEDIUM', true),
        ('11021111-0000-4000-8000-000000000015', 'Threshold frequency ν₀ for a metal is 7.0 × 10¹⁴ s⁻¹. Calculate kinetic energy of an electron emitted when radiation of frequency ν = 1.0 × 10¹⁵ s⁻¹ hits the metal.', NULL, NULL, 'SA', 'Chemistry', 'Structure of Atom', 3, 'KE = h(ν - ν₀) = 6.626×10⁻³⁴ × (10 - 7)×10¹⁴ = 1.988 × 10⁻¹⁹ J.', 'Formula (1M) + 1.988×10⁻¹⁹ J (2M)', NULL, 'KE = h(ν - ν₀) = 6.626×10⁻³⁴ × (10 - 7)×10¹⁴ = 1.988 × 10⁻¹⁹ J.', 'MEDIUM', true),
        ('11021111-0000-4000-8000-000000000016', 'Calculate wavelength and frequency of light emitted when an electron in H atom jumps from n = 4 to n = 2. (R_H = 1.097 × 10⁷ m⁻¹)', NULL, NULL, 'SA', 'Chemistry', 'Structure of Atom', 3, '1/λ = 1.097×10⁷ (1/4 - 1/16) = 1.097×10⁷ × 3/16 => λ = 486.2 nm (cyan-blue); ν = c/λ ≈ 6.17 × 10¹⁴ Hz.', 'Rydberg formula (1.5M) + 486 nm & frequency (1.5M)', NULL, '1/λ = 1.097×10⁷ (1/4 - 1/16) = 1.097×10⁷ × 3/16 => λ = 486.2 nm (cyan-blue); ν = c/λ ≈ 6.17 × 10¹⁴ Hz.', 'MEDIUM', true),
        ('11021111-0000-4000-8000-000000000017', 'List all four Quantum Numbers (n, l, m_l, m_s) and state the physical aspect each defines. Write electronic configurations of Cu (Z=29) and Fe²⁺ (Z=26), and find the total number of electrons in a 3p subshell vs total orbitals in n=4 shell.', NULL, NULL, 'LA', 'Chemistry', 'Structure of Atom', 5, '4 quantum numbers cleanly explained (2.5M); Cu: [Ar] 3d¹⁰ 4s¹ (0.75M); Fe²⁺: [Ar] 3d⁶ (0.75M); 3p max electrons = 6; orbitals in n=4 = n² = 16 (1M).', '4 Quantum numbers (2.5M) + Configs & counts (2.5M)', NULL, '4 quantum numbers cleanly explained (2.5M); Cu: [Ar] 3d¹⁰ 4s¹ (0.75M); Fe²⁺: [Ar] 3d⁶ (0.75M); 3p max electrons = 6; orbitals in n=4 = n² = 16 (1M).', 'HARD', true),
        ('11021111-0000-4000-8000-000000000018', '(i) Formula for Bohr orbit radius r_n and energy E_n of H atom. (ii) What is the ionisation energy of H from ground state (n=1)? (iii) Calculate de Broglie wavelength of a 0.1 kg ball moving at 10 m/s and say why wave nature is invisible macroscopically. (iv) Name two spectral series of Hydrogen in UV and IR.', NULL, NULL, 'CASE', 'Chemistry', 'Structure of Atom', 4, '(i) r_n = 0.529 n² Å; E_n = -13.6/n² eV. (ii) +13.6 eV (+2.18×10⁻¹⁸ J). (iii) λ = 6.626×10⁻³⁴ / 1 = 6.626×10⁻³⁴ m (too tiny to diffract). (iv) Lyman (UV), Paschen/Brackett/Pfund (IR).', '4 × 1M = 4M', 'Spectroscopists identify distant stellar compositions by matching crisp line spectra of atomic hydrogen against Niels Bohr''s quantised circular energy shells.', '(i) r_n = 0.529 n² Å; E_n = -13.6/n² eV. (ii) +13.6 eV (+2.18×10⁻¹⁸ J). (iii) λ = 6.626×10⁻³⁴ / 1 = 6.626×10⁻³⁴ m (too tiny to diffract). (iv) Lyman (UV), Paschen/Brackett/Pfund (IR).', 'HARD', true),
        ('11021111-0000-4000-8000-000000000019', 'Which element has the highest negative electron gain enthalpy (electron affinity) in the Periodic Table?', '{"A":"Fluorine (F)","B":"Chlorine (Cl)","C":"Oxygen (O)","D":"Bromine (Br)"}'::jsonb, 'B', 'MCQ', 'Chemistry', 'Classification of Elements and Periodicity in Properties', 1, 'Compact 2p shell of F causes e⁻-e⁻ repulsion, making Cl more negative (-349 kJ/mol).', NULL, NULL, 'Compact 2p shell of F causes e⁻-e⁻ repulsion, making Cl more negative (-349 kJ/mol).', 'EASY', true),
        ('11021111-0000-4000-8000-00000000001a', 'Correct increasing order of ionic radii among isoelectronic species N³⁻, O²⁻, F⁻, Na⁺, Mg²⁺ is:', '{"A":"N³⁻ < O²⁻ < F⁻ < Na⁺ < Mg²⁺","B":"Mg²⁺ < Na⁺ < F⁻ < O²⁻ < N³⁻","C":"F⁻ < O²⁻ < N³⁻ < Na⁺ < Mg²⁺","D":"Na⁺ < Mg²⁺ < F⁻ < O²⁻ < N³⁻"}'::jsonb, 'B', 'MCQ', 'Chemistry', 'Classification of Elements and Periodicity in Properties', 1, 'Higher nuclear charge Z pulls same 10 electrons closer -> smallest radius.', NULL, NULL, 'Higher nuclear charge Z pulls same 10 electrons closer -> smallest radius.', 'EASY', true),
        ('11021111-0000-4000-8000-00000000001b', 'First ionisation enthalpy of Nitrogen (N) is higher than that of Oxygen (O) because:', '{"A":"O is smaller","B":"N has extra-stable half-filled 2p³ configuration","C":"N has fewer protons","D":"O is a metal"}'::jsonb, 'B', 'MCQ', 'Chemistry', 'Classification of Elements and Periodicity in Properties', 1, '2p³ half-filled stability requires higher energy to remove an electron.', NULL, NULL, '2p³ half-filled stability requires higher energy to remove an electron.', 'EASY', true),
        ('11021111-0000-4000-8000-00000000001c', 'According to IUPAC systematic nomenclature, element with Z = 114 is named:', '{"A":"Unnilquadium","B":"Ununquadium (Flerovium)","C":"Ununpentium","D":"Unnilhexium"}'::jsonb, 'B', 'MCQ', 'Chemistry', 'Classification of Elements and Periodicity in Properties', 1, '1=un, 1=un, 4=quad + ium -> Ununquadium.', NULL, NULL, '1=un, 1=un, 4=quad + ium -> Ununquadium.', 'EASY', true),
        ('11021111-0000-4000-8000-00000000001d', 'Which oxide is amphoteric in nature?', '{"A":"Na₂O","B":"CO₂","C":"Al₂O₃","D":"Cl₂O₇"}'::jsonb, 'C', 'MCQ', 'Chemistry', 'Classification of Elements and Periodicity in Properties', 1, 'Al₂O₃ reacts with both acids and bases.', NULL, NULL, 'Al₂O₃ reacts with both acids and bases.', 'EASY', true),
        ('11021111-0000-4000-8000-00000000001e', 'Assertion (A): Atomic radius decreases across a period from left to right (excluding noble gases).
Reason (R): Electrons enter the same principal shell while effective nuclear charge increases, pulling valence electrons inward.
Choose the correct option:
A) Both A and R are true, and R is the correct explanation of A.
B) Both A and R are true, and R is not the correct explanation of A.
C) A is true but R is false.
D) A is false but R is true.', '{"A":"Both A and R are true, and R is the correct explanation of A","B":"Both A and R are true, and R is not the correct explanation of A","C":"A is true but R is false","D":"A is false but R is true"}'::jsonb, 'A', 'ASSERTION_REASON', 'Chemistry', 'Classification of Elements and Periodicity in Properties', 1, 'Higher Z_eff contracts atomic size.', NULL, NULL, 'Higher Z_eff contracts atomic size.', 'EASY', true),
        ('11021111-0000-4000-8000-00000000001f', 'Why are cations smaller and anions larger than their parent neutral atoms?', NULL, NULL, 'VSA', 'Chemistry', 'Classification of Elements and Periodicity in Properties', 2, 'Cation loses valence shell/electrons increasing nuclear pull per electron -> smaller. Anion gains electrons increasing inter-electronic repulsion and lowering Z_eff -> larger.', '1M each', NULL, 'Cation loses valence shell/electrons increasing nuclear pull per electron -> smaller. Anion gains electrons increasing inter-electronic repulsion and lowering Z_eff -> larger.', 'MEDIUM', true),
        ('11021111-0000-4000-8000-000000000020', 'Arrange B, Al, Mg, K in increasing order of metallic character with reason.', NULL, NULL, 'VSA', 'Chemistry', 'Classification of Elements and Periodicity in Properties', 2, 'B < Al < Mg < K. Metallic character increases down a group (B < Al) and decreases left-to-right across a period (K > Mg > Al).', 'Order (1M) + Reason (1M)', NULL, 'B < Al < Mg < K. Metallic character increases down a group (B < Al) and decreases left-to-right across a period (K > Mg > Al).', 'MEDIUM', true),
        ('11021111-0000-4000-8000-000000000021', 'Explain why: (i) Δ_iH₁ of Be > B; (ii) Δ_egH of O is less negative than S; (iii) Second ionisation enthalpy is always much greater than first.', NULL, NULL, 'SA', 'Chemistry', 'Classification of Elements and Periodicity in Properties', 3, '(i) Be loses from penetrating 2s² orbital vs B from higher-energy 2p¹. (ii) Small 2p orbital of O experiences strong inter-electronic repulsion compared to larger 3p of S. (iii) Removing e⁻ from positively charged unipositive ion faces stronger nuclear attraction.', '1M × 3', NULL, '(i) Be loses from penetrating 2s² orbital vs B from higher-energy 2p¹. (ii) Small 2p orbital of O experiences strong inter-electronic repulsion compared to larger 3p of S. (iii) Removing e⁻ from positively charged unipositive ion faces stronger nuclear attraction.', 'MEDIUM', true),
        ('11021111-0000-4000-8000-000000000022', 'What is Diagonal Relationship? Give two pairs of diagonally related elements and two similar properties between Li and Mg.', NULL, NULL, 'SA', 'Chemistry', 'Classification of Elements and Periodicity in Properties', 3, 'Similar properties between Period-2 element and Period-3 diagonally lower-right element due to comparable charge/radius ratio (polarising power). Pairs: (Li, Mg), (Be, Al), (B, Si). Li & Mg form nitrides (Li₃N, Mg₃N₂), less soluble carbonates/fluorides, and don''t form solid bicarbonates.', 'Definition & pairs (1.5M) + Li-Mg similarities (1.5M)', NULL, 'Similar properties between Period-2 element and Period-3 diagonally lower-right element due to comparable charge/radius ratio (polarising power). Pairs: (Li, Mg), (Be, Al), (B, Si). Li & Mg form nitrides (Li₃N, Mg₃N₂), less soluble carbonates/fluorides, and don''t form solid bicarbonates.', 'MEDIUM', true),
        ('11021111-0000-4000-8000-000000000023', 'Explain periodic trends across a period and down a group for: (i) Ionisation Enthalpy, (ii) Electron Gain Enthalpy, (iii) Electronegativity, (iv) Metallic & Non-metallic character. Predict block, period, and group of element with outer config [Ar] 3d⁵ 4s².', NULL, NULL, 'LA', 'Chemistry', 'Classification of Elements and Periodicity in Properties', 5, '4 clear periodic trends (4M). [Ar] 3d⁵ 4s² -> Z=25 (Mn): d-block, Period 4, Group 7 (1M).', 'Trends (4M) + Element block/period/group (1M)', NULL, '4 clear periodic trends (4M). [Ar] 3d⁵ 4s² -> Z=25 (Mn): d-block, Period 4, Group 7 (1M).', 'HARD', true),
        ('11021111-0000-4000-8000-000000000024', '(i) Identify the reactive alkali metal and halogen based on Δ_iH₁, Δ_iH₂ values. (ii) Which element is likely a noble gas? (iii) Which forms a stable dihalide MX₂? (iv) Name the most electronegative element overall.', NULL, NULL, 'CASE', 'Chemistry', 'Classification of Elements and Periodicity in Properties', 4, '(i) Lowest Δ_iH₁ + huge Δ_iH₂ jump = Alkali metal (ns¹); very high negative Δ_egH = Halogen. (ii) Highest Δ_iH₁ + positive Δ_egH = Noble gas. (iii) Alkaline earth metal (low Δ_iH₁ & Δ_iH₂, huge Δ_iH₃). (iv) Fluorine (Pauling 4.0).', '4 × 1M = 4M', 'Analysing successive ionisation enthalpies (Δ_iH₁, Δ_iH₂, Δ_iH₃) and electron gain enthalpies reveals an element''s valence shell count and chemical reactivity.', '(i) Lowest Δ_iH₁ + huge Δ_iH₂ jump = Alkali metal (ns¹); very high negative Δ_egH = Halogen. (ii) Highest Δ_iH₁ + positive Δ_egH = Noble gas. (iii) Alkaline earth metal (low Δ_iH₁ & Δ_iH₂, huge Δ_iH₃). (iv) Fluorine (Pauling 4.0).', 'HARD', true),
        ('11021111-0000-4000-8000-000000000025', 'According to VSEPR theory, the geometry and bond angle of SF₆ molecule are:', '{"A":"Trigonal bipyramidal, 90° & 120°","B":"Octahedral, 90°","C":"Tetrahedral, 109.5°","D":"Square planar, 90°"}'::jsonb, 'B', 'MCQ', 'Chemistry', 'Chemical Bonding and Molecular Structure', 1, '6 bond pairs, 0 lone pairs -> sp³d² Octahedral (90°).', NULL, NULL, '6 bond pairs, 0 lone pairs -> sp³d² Octahedral (90°).', 'EASY', true),
        ('11021111-0000-4000-8000-000000000026', 'Hybridisation of central carbon in ethyne (C₂H₂) and ethene (C₂H₄) respectively is:', '{"A":"sp² and sp³","B":"sp and sp²","C":"sp³ and sp²","D":"sp and sp³"}'::jsonb, 'B', 'MCQ', 'Chemistry', 'Chemical Bonding and Molecular Structure', 1, 'Triple bond C = sp (linear); double bond C = sp² (trigonal planar).', NULL, NULL, 'Triple bond C = sp (linear); double bond C = sp² (trigonal planar).', 'EASY', true),
        ('11021111-0000-4000-8000-000000000027', 'Using Molecular Orbital Theory, bond order and magnetic behaviour of O₂ are:', '{"A":"2, Diamagnetic","B":"2, Paramagnetic (2 unpaired electrons in π*2p)","C":"2.5, Paramagnetic","D":"1, Diamagnetic"}'::jsonb, 'B', 'MCQ', 'Chemistry', 'Chemical Bonding and Molecular Structure', 1, 'Two unpaired electrons in π*2p_x and π*2p_y make O₂ paramagnetic.', NULL, NULL, 'Two unpaired electrons in π*2p_x and π*2p_y make O₂ paramagnetic.', 'EASY', true),
        ('11021111-0000-4000-8000-000000000028', 'Why does NH₃ have a much higher dipole moment (1.47 D) than NF₃ (0.23 D) despite same trigonal pyramidal shape?', '{"A":"N–H bond is ionic","B":"In NH₃ orbital lone-pair dipole adds to N–H bond dipoles, whereas in NF₃ it opposes N–F dipoles","C":"NF₃ is linear","D":"H is more electronegative than F"}'::jsonb, 'B', 'MCQ', 'Chemistry', 'Chemical Bonding and Molecular Structure', 1, 'Reinforcing vs opposing dipole vectors.', NULL, NULL, 'Reinforcing vs opposing dipole vectors.', 'EASY', true),
        ('11021111-0000-4000-8000-000000000029', 'Which hydrogen bond exists within the same molecule (intramolecular H-bonding)?', '{"A":"p-Nitrophenol","B":"o-Nitrophenol","C":"H₂O","D":"NH₃"}'::jsonb, 'B', 'MCQ', 'Chemistry', 'Chemical Bonding and Molecular Structure', 1, 'Proximity of -OH and -NO₂ at ortho positions allows intramolecular ring H-bond.', NULL, NULL, 'Proximity of -OH and -NO₂ at ortho positions allows intramolecular ring H-bond.', 'EASY', true),
        ('11021111-0000-4000-8000-00000000002a', 'Assertion (A): CO₂ molecule has zero net dipole moment even though C=O bonds are polar.
Reason (R): CO₂ is linear (O=C=O), so the two equal and opposite bond dipoles cancel out vectorially.
Choose the correct option:
A) Both A and R are true, and R is the correct explanation of A.
B) Both A and R are true, and R is not the correct explanation of A.
C) A is true but R is false.
D) A is false but R is true.', '{"A":"Both A and R are true, and R is the correct explanation of A","B":"Both A and R are true, and R is not the correct explanation of A","C":"A is true but R is false","D":"A is false but R is true"}'::jsonb, 'A', 'ASSERTION_REASON', 'Chemistry', 'Chemical Bonding and Molecular Structure', 1, 'Symmetric linear geometry cancels bond dipoles.', NULL, NULL, 'Symmetric linear geometry cancels bond dipoles.', 'EASY', true),
        ('11021111-0000-4000-8000-00000000002b', 'Why is H₂O a liquid at room temperature whereas H₂S is a gas?', NULL, NULL, 'VSA', 'Chemistry', 'Chemical Bonding and Molecular Structure', 2, 'Oxygen''s high electronegativity and small size enable strong intermolecular hydrogen bonding among H₂O molecules, whereas larger S cannot form effective H-bonds in H₂S.', 'Intermolecular H-bonding explanation (2M)', NULL, 'Oxygen''s high electronegativity and small size enable strong intermolecular hydrogen bonding among H₂O molecules, whereas larger S cannot form effective H-bonds in H₂S.', 'MEDIUM', true),
        ('11021111-0000-4000-8000-00000000002c', 'Compare bond angles in CH₄ (109.5°), NH₃ (107°), and H₂O (104.5°) using VSEPR theory.', NULL, NULL, 'VSA', 'Chemistry', 'Chemical Bonding and Molecular Structure', 2, 'All have sp³ hybridisation (4 electron pairs). Increasing lone pairs (0 -> 1 -> 2) exerts stronger lp-lp > lp-bp > bp-bp repulsion, squeezing bond angles down from 109.5° to 107° to 104.5°.', 'Repulsion order + Lone pair count (2M)', NULL, 'All have sp³ hybridisation (4 electron pairs). Increasing lone pairs (0 -> 1 -> 2) exerts stronger lp-lp > lp-bp > bp-bp repulsion, squeezing bond angles down from 109.5° to 107° to 104.5°.', 'MEDIUM', true),
        ('11021111-0000-4000-8000-00000000002d', 'Draw/describe shapes and hybridisations of (i) PCl₅, (ii) SF₄, and (iii) BrF₅ using VSEPR theory.', NULL, NULL, 'SA', 'Chemistry', 'Chemical Bonding and Molecular Structure', 3, '(i) PCl₅: 5 bp, 0 lp -> sp³d Trigonal bipyramidal. (ii) SF₄: 4 bp, 1 lp -> sp³d See-saw. (iii) BrF₅: 5 bp, 1 lp -> sp³d² Square pyramidal.', '1M × 3', NULL, '(i) PCl₅: 5 bp, 0 lp -> sp³d Trigonal bipyramidal. (ii) SF₄: 4 bp, 1 lp -> sp³d See-saw. (iii) BrF₅: 5 bp, 1 lp -> sp³d² Square pyramidal.', 'MEDIUM', true),
        ('11021111-0000-4000-8000-00000000002e', 'Arrange N₂, N₂⁺, N₂⁻, N₂²⁻ in decreasing order of bond stability using Molecular Orbital Theory and justify.', NULL, NULL, 'SA', 'Chemistry', 'Chemical Bonding and Molecular Structure', 3, 'Bond orders: N₂ = 3.0; N₂⁺ = 2.5; N₂⁻ = 2.5; N₂²⁻ = 2.0. N₂⁺ is slightly more stable than N₂⁻ (fewer antibonding electrons). Order: N₂ > N₂⁺ > N₂⁻ > N₂²⁻.', 'Bond order calcs (2M) + Stability order (1M)', NULL, 'Bond orders: N₂ = 3.0; N₂⁺ = 2.5; N₂⁻ = 2.5; N₂²⁻ = 2.0. N₂⁺ is slightly more stable than N₂⁻ (fewer antibonding electrons). Order: N₂ > N₂⁺ > N₂⁻ > N₂²⁻.', 'MEDIUM', true),
        ('11021111-0000-4000-8000-00000000002f', 'Draw/write the Molecular Orbital Energy Level configuration of O₂, O₂⁺, O₂⁻, and O₂²⁻ (Z=8). Calculate each species'' bond order, state magnetic character, and arrange them in increasing order of bond length.', NULL, NULL, 'LA', 'Chemistry', 'Chemical Bonding and Molecular Structure', 5, 'O₂ (BO=2.0, Para), O₂⁺ (BO=2.5, Para), O₂⁻ (BO=1.5, Para), O₂²⁻ (BO=1.0, Dia). Bond length is inversely proportional to bond order: O₂⁺ < O₂ < O₂⁻ < O₂²⁻.', '4 MO configs + BO + magnetism (3.5M) + Bond length order (1.5M)', NULL, 'O₂ (BO=2.0, Para), O₂⁺ (BO=2.5, Para), O₂⁻ (BO=1.5, Para), O₂²⁻ (BO=1.0, Dia). Bond length is inversely proportional to bond order: O₂⁺ < O₂ < O₂⁻ < O₂²⁻.', 'HARD', true),
        ('11021111-0000-4000-8000-000000000030', '(i) State Fajan''s rules predicting covalent character in ionic bonds. (ii) Between LiCl and KCl, which has higher covalent character? (iii) How many σ and π bonds are in tetracyanoethylene (CN)₂C=C(CN)₂? (iv) Why is axial P–Cl bond longer than equatorial P–Cl in PCl₅?', NULL, NULL, 'CASE', 'Chemistry', 'Chemical Bonding and Molecular Structure', 4, '(i) Small cation, large anion, high charge -> greater polarisation/covalent character. (ii) LiCl (smaller Li⁺ polarises Cl⁻ strongly). (iii) 9 σ bonds + 9 π bonds. (iv) Axial pairs face three 90° bond-pair repulsions whereas equatorial face two.', '4 × 1M = 4M', 'Modern chemistry bridges purely ionic lattices and covalent orbital sharing through Fajan''s polarisation rules and sp–sp³ hybrid orbital overlaps.', '(i) Small cation, large anion, high charge -> greater polarisation/covalent character. (ii) LiCl (smaller Li⁺ polarises Cl⁻ strongly). (iii) 9 σ bonds + 9 π bonds. (iv) Axial pairs face three 90° bond-pair repulsions whereas equatorial face two.', 'HARD', true),
        ('11021111-0000-4000-8000-000000000031', 'For a process to be spontaneous at all temperatures, what must be the signs of ΔH and ΔS?', '{"A":"ΔH > 0, ΔS < 0","B":"ΔH < 0, ΔS > 0","C":"ΔH > 0, ΔS > 0","D":"ΔH < 0, ΔS < 0"}'::jsonb, 'B', 'MCQ', 'Chemistry', 'Chemical Thermodynamics', 1, 'ΔG = ΔH - TΔS becomes strictly negative when ΔH < 0 and ΔS > 0.', NULL, NULL, 'ΔG = ΔH - TΔS becomes strictly negative when ΔH < 0 and ΔS > 0.', 'EASY', true),
        ('11021111-0000-4000-8000-000000000032', 'Enthalpy of combustion of methane is always:', '{"A":"Positive","B":"Negative","C":"Zero","D":"Equal to ΔS"}'::jsonb, 'B', 'MCQ', 'Chemistry', 'Chemical Thermodynamics', 1, 'Combustion reactions are always exothermic (ΔH < 0).', NULL, NULL, 'Combustion reactions are always exothermic (ΔH < 0).', 'EASY', true),
        ('11021111-0000-4000-8000-000000000033', 'Relationship between ΔH and ΔU for a gaseous reaction is:', '{"A":"ΔH = ΔU - Δn_g RT","B":"ΔH = ΔU + Δn_g RT","C":"ΔH = ΔU × Δn_g","D":"ΔH = ΔU"}'::jsonb, 'B', 'MCQ', 'Chemistry', 'Chemical Thermodynamics', 1, 'ΔH = ΔU + Δn_g RT.', NULL, NULL, 'ΔH = ΔU + Δn_g RT.', 'EASY', true),
        ('11021111-0000-4000-8000-000000000034', 'Which property is an INTENSIVE state function?', '{"A":"Enthalpy H","B":"Volume V","C":"Molar heat capacity / Temperature / Pressure","D":"Internal energy U"}'::jsonb, 'C', 'MCQ', 'Chemistry', 'Chemical Thermodynamics', 1, 'Molar properties & T, P don''t scale with system mass.', NULL, NULL, 'Molar properties & T, P don''t scale with system mass.', 'EASY', true),
        ('11021111-0000-4000-8000-000000000035', 'Standard enthalpy of formation Δ_fH° of an element in its most stable reference state (e.g., O₂(g), C(graphite)) is:', '{"A":"+100 kJ/mol","B":"Zero","C":"-100 kJ/mol","D":"Undefined"}'::jsonb, 'B', 'MCQ', 'Chemistry', 'Chemical Thermodynamics', 1, 'By convention Δ_fH° = 0 for reference states.', NULL, NULL, 'By convention Δ_fH° = 0 for reference states.', 'EASY', true),
        ('11021111-0000-4000-8000-000000000036', 'Assertion (A): Entropy of the universe steadily increases during every spontaneous natural process.
Reason (R): All natural processes are thermodynamically irreversible and increase overall disorder (ΔS_total > 0).
Choose the correct option:
A) Both A and R are true, and R is the correct explanation of A.
B) Both A and R are true, and R is not the correct explanation of A.
C) A is true but R is false.
D) A is false but R is true.', '{"A":"Both A and R are true, and R is the correct explanation of A","B":"Both A and R are true, and R is not the correct explanation of A","C":"A is true but R is false","D":"A is false but R is true"}'::jsonb, 'A', 'ASSERTION_REASON', 'Chemistry', 'Chemical Thermodynamics', 1, 'Second law statement for isolated universe.', NULL, NULL, 'Second law statement for isolated universe.', 'EASY', true),
        ('11021111-0000-4000-8000-000000000037', 'State Hess''s Law of Constant Heat Summation with one application.', NULL, NULL, 'VSA', 'Chemistry', 'Chemical Thermodynamics', 2, 'Total enthalpy change of a reaction is the same whether it occurs in one step or several intermediate steps. Used to find ΔH of slow/unmeasurable reactions like C(graphite) + ½O₂ -> CO.', 'Law (1M) + Application (1M)', NULL, 'Total enthalpy change of a reaction is the same whether it occurs in one step or several intermediate steps. Used to find ΔH of slow/unmeasurable reactions like C(graphite) + ½O₂ -> CO.', 'MEDIUM', true),
        ('11021111-0000-4000-8000-000000000038', 'Define Standard Enthalpy of Atomisation and Bond Dissociation Enthalpy.', NULL, NULL, 'VSA', 'Chemistry', 'Chemical Thermodynamics', 2, 'Atomisation: enthalpy change breaking 1 mole of a substance completely into gaseous atoms. Bond dissociation: breaking 1 mole of a particular bond in a gaseous molecule.', '1M each', NULL, 'Atomisation: enthalpy change breaking 1 mole of a substance completely into gaseous atoms. Bond dissociation: breaking 1 mole of a particular bond in a gaseous molecule.', 'MEDIUM', true),
        ('11021111-0000-4000-8000-000000000039', 'For the reaction 2A(g) + B(g) -> 2D(g), ΔU° = -10.5 kJ and ΔS° = -44.1 J/K at 298 K. Calculate ΔG° and predict spontaneity.', NULL, NULL, 'SA', 'Chemistry', 'Chemical Thermodynamics', 3, 'Δn_g = 2 - 3 = -1. ΔH° = -10.5 + (-1)(8.314×10⁻³)(298) = -10.5 - 2.48 = -12.98 kJ. ΔG° = -12.98 - 298(-0.0441) = -12.98 + 13.14 = +0.16 kJ (> 0 -> Non-spontaneous).', 'ΔH° (1.5M) + ΔG°=+0.16 kJ & non-spontaneous (1.5M)', NULL, 'Δn_g = 2 - 3 = -1. ΔH° = -10.5 + (-1)(8.314×10⁻³)(298) = -10.5 - 2.48 = -12.98 kJ. ΔG° = -12.98 - 298(-0.0441) = -12.98 + 13.14 = +0.16 kJ (> 0 -> Non-spontaneous).', 'MEDIUM', true),
        ('11021111-0000-4000-8000-00000000003a', 'Calculate the standard enthalpy of formation of CH₃OH(l) from: CH₃OH(l) + 3/2 O₂ -> CO₂ + 2H₂O (ΔH=-726 kJ), C + O₂ -> CO₂ (-393 kJ), H₂ + ½O₂ -> H₂O (-286 kJ).', NULL, NULL, 'SA', 'Chemistry', 'Chemical Thermodynamics', 3, 'Reverse 1st + eq(2) + 2×eq(3) -> C + 2H₂ + ½O₂ -> CH₃OH(l): ΔH = -393 + 2(-286) - (-726) = -965 + 726 = -239 kJ/mol.', 'Hess cycle setup (1.5M) + -239 kJ/mol (1.5M)', NULL, 'Reverse 1st + eq(2) + 2×eq(3) -> C + 2H₂ + ½O₂ -> CH₃OH(l): ΔH = -393 + 2(-286) - (-726) = -965 + 726 = -239 kJ/mol.', 'MEDIUM', true),
        ('11021111-0000-4000-8000-00000000003b', 'Derive the Gibbs-Helmholtz equation ΔG = ΔH - TΔS from ΔS_total = ΔS_sys + ΔS_surr > 0. Discuss all four temperature conditions determining spontaneity when (i) ΔH < 0, ΔS > 0, (ii) ΔH > 0, ΔS < 0, (iii) ΔH > 0, ΔS > 0, (iv) ΔH < 0, ΔS < 0.', NULL, NULL, 'LA', 'Chemistry', 'Chemical Thermodynamics', 5, 'Derivation ΔS_surr = -ΔH/T -> -TΔS_total = ΔH - TΔS = ΔG < 0 (2.5M). Four cases: always spontaneous, never spontaneous, spontaneous at high T (> ΔH/ΔS), spontaneous at low T (< ΔH/ΔS) (2.5M).', 'Derivation (2.5M) + 4 cases (2.5M)', NULL, 'Derivation ΔS_surr = -ΔH/T -> -TΔS_total = ΔH - TΔS = ΔG < 0 (2.5M). Four cases: always spontaneous, never spontaneous, spontaneous at high T (> ΔH/ΔS), spontaneous at low T (< ΔH/ΔS) (2.5M).', 'HARD', true),
        ('11021111-0000-4000-8000-00000000003c', '(i) Sign of ΔH and ΔS during melting of ice above 0°C. (ii) Calculate ΔS_vap when 1 mole H₂O boils at 373 K with Δ_vapH = 40.66 kJ/mol. (iii) Relation between ΔG° and equilibrium constant K. (iv) If equilibrium constant K = 10 at 300 K, find ΔG° sign.', NULL, NULL, 'CASE', 'Chemistry', 'Chemical Thermodynamics', 4, '(i) ΔH > 0 (endothermic), ΔS > 0 (solid -> liquid). (ii) 40660 / 373 = 109 J/(mol K). (iii) ΔG° = -RT ln K = -2.303 RT log K. (iv) K > 1 => ΔG° is negative (spontaneous forward).', '4 × 1M = 4M', 'A bomb calorimeter experiment logs enthalpy changes during phase transitions and links standard Gibbs free energy directly to chemical equilibrium yield.', '(i) ΔH > 0 (endothermic), ΔS > 0 (solid -> liquid). (ii) 40660 / 373 = 109 J/(mol K). (iii) ΔG° = -RT ln K = -2.303 RT log K. (iv) K > 1 => ΔG° is negative (spontaneous forward).', 'HARD', true),
        ('11021111-0000-4000-8000-00000000003d', 'Relationship between K_p and K_c for a gaseous reversible reaction is:', '{"A":"K_p = K_c (RT)^(-Δn_g)","B":"K_p = K_c (RT)^(Δn_g)","C":"K_p = K_c + RT","D":"K_p = K_c"}'::jsonb, 'B', 'MCQ', 'Chemistry', 'Equilibrium', 1, 'K_p = K_c (RT)^(Δn_g).', NULL, NULL, 'K_p = K_c (RT)^(Δn_g).', 'EASY', true),
        ('11021111-0000-4000-8000-00000000003e', 'Conjugate acid and conjugate base of H₂O respectively are:', '{"A":"OH⁻ and H₃O⁺","B":"H₃O⁺ and OH⁻","C":"O²⁻ and H₂","D":"H⁺ and H₂O"}'::jsonb, 'B', 'MCQ', 'Chemistry', 'Equilibrium', 1, 'Gain H⁺ -> H₃O⁺ (conj. acid); lose H⁺ -> OH⁻ (conj. base).', NULL, NULL, 'Gain H⁺ -> H₃O⁺ (conj. acid); lose H⁺ -> OH⁻ (conj. base).', 'EASY', true),
        ('11021111-0000-4000-8000-00000000003f', 'At 25°C, pH of 0.001 M HCl solution is:', '{"A":"1","B":"2","C":"3","D":"11"}'::jsonb, 'C', 'MCQ', 'Chemistry', 'Equilibrium', 1, '-log(10⁻³) = 3.', NULL, NULL, '-log(10⁻³) = 3.', 'EASY', true),
        ('11021111-0000-4000-8000-000000000040', 'According to Le Chatelier''s Principle, increasing pressure shifts N₂(g) + 3H₂(g) ⇌ 2NH₃(g) toward:', '{"A":"Reactant side (4 moles)","B":"Product side (2 moles, forward)","C":"No shift","D":"Equilibrium constant increases"}'::jsonb, 'B', 'MCQ', 'Chemistry', 'Equilibrium', 1, 'Shifts toward fewer gaseous moles (4 -> 2).', NULL, NULL, 'Shifts toward fewer gaseous moles (4 -> 2).', 'EASY', true),
        ('11021111-0000-4000-8000-000000000041', 'A mixture of CH₃COOH and CH₃COONa acts as:', '{"A":"Basic buffer","B":"Acidic buffer resisting pH change","C":"Strong electrolyte","D":"Neutral salt"}'::jsonb, 'B', 'MCQ', 'Chemistry', 'Equilibrium', 1, 'Weak acid + salt with strong base forms an acidic buffer.', NULL, NULL, 'Weak acid + salt with strong base forms an acidic buffer.', 'EASY', true),
        ('11021111-0000-4000-8000-000000000042', 'Assertion (A): Solubility of AgCl decreases sharply when NaCl is added to its saturated aqueous solution.
Reason (R): Presence of common Cl⁻ ions shifts AgCl ⇌ Ag⁺ + Cl⁻ backward by Le Chatelier''s Principle (Common Ion Effect).
Choose the correct option:
A) Both A and R are true, and R is the correct explanation of A.
B) Both A and R are true, and R is not the correct explanation of A.
C) A is true but R is false.
D) A is false but R is true.', '{"A":"Both A and R are true, and R is the correct explanation of A","B":"Both A and R are true, and R is not the correct explanation of A","C":"A is true but R is false","D":"A is false but R is true"}'::jsonb, 'A', 'ASSERTION_REASON', 'Chemistry', 'Equilibrium', 1, 'Classic Common Ion Effect.', NULL, NULL, 'Classic Common Ion Effect.', 'EASY', true),
        ('11021111-0000-4000-8000-000000000043', 'What is Buffer Solution? Give one example each of an acidic and a basic buffer.', NULL, NULL, 'VSA', 'Chemistry', 'Equilibrium', 2, 'Solution resisting drastic pH change upon dilution or small acid/base addition. Acidic: CH₃COOH + CH₃COONa; Basic: NH₄OH + NH₄Cl.', 'Definition (1M) + Examples (1M)', NULL, 'Solution resisting drastic pH change upon dilution or small acid/base addition. Acidic: CH₃COOH + CH₃COONa; Basic: NH₄OH + NH₄Cl.', 'MEDIUM', true),
        ('11021111-0000-4000-8000-000000000044', 'Write the conjugate base of HF, H₂SO₄, HCO₃⁻ and conjugate acid of NH₃.', NULL, NULL, 'VSA', 'Chemistry', 'Equilibrium', 2, 'F⁻, HSO₄⁻, CO₃²⁻; NH₄⁺.', '0.5M × 4 = 2M', NULL, 'F⁻, HSO₄⁻, CO₃²⁻; NH₄⁺.', 'MEDIUM', true),
        ('11021111-0000-4000-8000-000000000045', 'For N₂(g) + 3H₂(g) ⇌ 2NH₃(g) (ΔH = -92.3 kJ), state effect on NH₃ yield upon: (i) increasing pressure, (ii) increasing temperature, (iii) adding catalyst.', NULL, NULL, 'SA', 'Chemistry', 'Equilibrium', 3, '(i) High pressure favours forward (fewer moles -> higher NH₃). (ii) Exothermic -> raising T shifts backward (lower NH₃). (iii) Catalyst speeds up both forward & reverse equally; no shift in equilibrium yield.', '1M × 3', NULL, '(i) High pressure favours forward (fewer moles -> higher NH₃). (ii) Exothermic -> raising T shifts backward (lower NH₃). (iii) Catalyst speeds up both forward & reverse equally; no shift in equilibrium yield.', 'MEDIUM', true),
        ('11021111-0000-4000-8000-000000000046', 'Calculate pH of a 0.02 M Ba(OH)₂ aqueous solution assuming complete dissociation at 298 K. (log 4 = 0.602)', NULL, NULL, 'SA', 'Chemistry', 'Equilibrium', 3, 'Ba(OH)₂ -> Ba²⁺ + 2OH⁻ => [OH⁻] = 2×0.02 = 0.04 = 4×10⁻² M. pOH = 2 - 0.602 = 1.398. pH = 14 - 1.398 = 12.60.', '[OH⁻]=0.04 (1M) + pOH (1M) + pH=12.60 (1M)', NULL, 'Ba(OH)₂ -> Ba²⁺ + 2OH⁻ => [OH⁻] = 2×0.02 = 0.04 = 4×10⁻² M. pOH = 2 - 0.602 = 1.398. pH = 14 - 1.398 = 12.60.', 'MEDIUM', true),
        ('11021111-0000-4000-8000-000000000047', 'Define Solubility Product (K_sp). Derive relation between K_sp and molar solubility s for (i) Ag₂CrO₄ and (ii) Ca₃(PO₄)₂. If K_sp of AgCl is 1.8 × 10⁻¹⁰, calculate its molar solubility in pure water vs 0.1 M NaCl.', NULL, NULL, 'LA', 'Chemistry', 'Equilibrium', 5, 'K_sp definition (1M). (i) Ag₂CrO₄ -> 2Ag⁺ + CrO₄²⁻ => K_sp = (2s)²(s) = 4s³ (1M). (ii) Ca₃(PO₄)₂ => (3s)³(2s)² = 108s⁵ (1M). Pure water: s = √(1.8×10⁻¹⁰) = 1.34×10⁻⁵ M; in 0.1 M NaCl: s'' = 1.8×10⁻¹⁰ / 0.1 = 1.8×10⁻⁹ M (2M).', 'K_sp & relations (3M) + Numerical (2M)', NULL, 'K_sp definition (1M). (i) Ag₂CrO₄ -> 2Ag⁺ + CrO₄²⁻ => K_sp = (2s)²(s) = 4s³ (1M). (ii) Ca₃(PO₄)₂ => (3s)³(2s)² = 108s⁵ (1M). Pure water: s = √(1.8×10⁻¹⁰) = 1.34×10⁻⁵ M; in 0.1 M NaCl: s'' = 1.8×10⁻¹⁰ / 0.1 = 1.8×10⁻⁹ M (2M).', 'HARD', true),
        ('11021111-0000-4000-8000-000000000048', '(i) Compare Reaction Quotient Q_c with K_c when Q_c < K_c. (ii) Value of K_p when Δn_g = 0 (like H₂+I₂ ⇌ 2HI). (iii) Why is pH of pure water 6.8 (not 7.0) at 310 K? (iv) Name Lewis acid and Lewis base in BF₃ + :NH₃ -> F₃B<-NH₃.', NULL, NULL, 'CASE', 'Chemistry', 'Equilibrium', 4, '(i) Forward reaction predominates until Q_c = K_c. (ii) K_p = K_c. (iii) Endothermic auto-ionisation increases K_w > 10⁻¹⁴ at higher T, lowering neutral pH below 7 (still neutral since [H⁺]=[OH⁻]). (iv) BF₃ = Lewis acid (e⁻ acceptor); NH₃ = Lewis base (e⁻ donor).', '4 × 1M = 4M', 'Industrial Haber-Bosch reactors balance thermodynamics and kinetics while monitoring Q_c vs K_c and common-ion shifts in salt precipitation baths.', '(i) Forward reaction predominates until Q_c = K_c. (ii) K_p = K_c. (iii) Endothermic auto-ionisation increases K_w > 10⁻¹⁴ at higher T, lowering neutral pH below 7 (still neutral since [H⁺]=[OH⁻]). (iv) BF₃ = Lewis acid (e⁻ acceptor); NH₃ = Lewis base (e⁻ donor).', 'HARD', true),
        ('11021111-0000-4000-8000-000000000049', 'Oxidation number of Cr in Cr₂O₇²⁻ and Mn in MnO₄⁻ respectively are:', '{"A":"+6 and +7","B":"+7 and +6","C":"+3 and +4","D":"+6 and +5"}'::jsonb, 'A', 'MCQ', 'Chemistry', 'Redox Reactions', 1, '2x - 14 = -2 => +6; x - 8 = -1 => +7.', NULL, NULL, '2x - 14 = -2 => +6; x - 8 = -1 => +7.', 'EASY', true),
        ('11021111-0000-4000-8000-00000000004a', 'Which reaction is a disproportionation reaction?', '{"A":"2H₂ + O₂ -> 2H₂O","B":"Cl₂ + 2OH⁻ -> Cl⁻ + ClO⁻ + H₂O","C":"Zn + CuSO₄ -> ZnSO₄ + Cu","D":"CaCO₃ -> CaO + CO₂"}'::jsonb, 'B', 'MCQ', 'Chemistry', 'Redox Reactions', 1, 'Cl₂ (0) is simultaneously reduced to Cl⁻ (-1) and oxidised to ClO⁻ (+1).', NULL, NULL, 'Cl₂ (0) is simultaneously reduced to Cl⁻ (-1) and oxidised to ClO⁻ (+1).', 'EASY', true),
        ('11021111-0000-4000-8000-00000000004b', 'Oxidation number of Fe in Fe₃O₄ (mixed oxide) is:', '{"A":"+2","B":"+3","C":"+8/3","D":"+4"}'::jsonb, 'C', 'MCQ', 'Chemistry', 'Redox Reactions', 1, '3x - 8 = 0 => +8/3 (one Fe²⁺ and two Fe³⁺).', NULL, NULL, '3x - 8 = 0 => +8/3 (one Fe²⁺ and two Fe³⁺).', 'EASY', true),
        ('11021111-0000-4000-8000-00000000004c', 'In a Daniell galvanic cell (Zn | Zn²⁺ || Cu²⁺ | Cu), electrons flow externally from:', '{"A":"Copper to Zinc","B":"Zinc anode to Copper cathode","C":"Salt bridge to Zn","D":"Solution to Cu"}'::jsonb, 'B', 'MCQ', 'Chemistry', 'Redox Reactions', 1, 'Zn oxidises releasing e⁻ which flow from anode (Zn) to cathode (Cu).', NULL, NULL, 'Zn oxidises releasing e⁻ which flow from anode (Zn) to cathode (Cu).', 'EASY', true),
        ('11021111-0000-4000-8000-00000000004d', 'Stock notation for Iron(III) chloride is:', '{"A":"FeCl₃(III)","B":"Fe(III)Cl₃","C":"III-FeCl₃","D":"Fe₃Cl"}'::jsonb, 'B', 'MCQ', 'Chemistry', 'Redox Reactions', 1, 'Roman numeral in parentheses after metal symbol.', NULL, NULL, 'Roman numeral in parentheses after metal symbol.', 'EASY', true),
        ('11021111-0000-4000-8000-00000000004e', 'Assertion (A): Fluorine always exhibits an oxidation state of -1 in all its compounds.
Reason (R): Fluorine is the most electronegative element and lacks d-orbitals to expand beyond -1.
Choose the correct option:
A) Both A and R are true, and R is the correct explanation of A.
B) Both A and R are true, and R is not the correct explanation of A.
C) A is true but R is false.
D) A is false but R is true.', '{"A":"Both A and R are true, and R is the correct explanation of A","B":"Both A and R are true, and R is not the correct explanation of A","C":"A is true but R is false","D":"A is false but R is true"}'::jsonb, 'A', 'ASSERTION_REASON', 'Chemistry', 'Redox Reactions', 1, 'F cannot take positive oxidation state.', NULL, NULL, 'F cannot take positive oxidation state.', 'EASY', true),
        ('11021111-0000-4000-8000-00000000004f', 'Define Disproportionation reaction with one balanced equation. Why cannot F₂ show disproportionation?', NULL, NULL, 'VSA', 'Chemistry', 'Redox Reactions', 2, 'A single element in an intermediate oxidation state simultaneously undergoes oxidation and reduction (e.g., 2H₂O₂ -> 2H₂O + O₂). F₂ is most electronegative and cannot show positive oxidation states (+ve), so it cannot disproportionate.', 'Definition + equation (1M) + F₂ reason (1M)', NULL, 'A single element in an intermediate oxidation state simultaneously undergoes oxidation and reduction (e.g., 2H₂O₂ -> 2H₂O + O₂). F₂ is most electronegative and cannot show positive oxidation states (+ve), so it cannot disproportionate.', 'MEDIUM', true),
        ('11021111-0000-4000-8000-000000000050', 'Find oxidation states of (i) S in H₂SO₅ (Caro''s acid) and (ii) N in NO₃⁻.', NULL, NULL, 'VSA', 'Chemistry', 'Redox Reactions', 2, '(i) Two oxygens form peroxide -O-O- linkage (-1 each), remaining three are -2: 2(+1) + x + 3(-2) + 2(-1) = 0 => x = +6. (ii) x - 6 = -1 => x = +5.', '1M each', NULL, '(i) Two oxygens form peroxide -O-O- linkage (-1 each), remaining three are -2: 2(+1) + x + 3(-2) + 2(-1) = 0 => x = +6. (ii) x - 6 = -1 => x = +5.', 'MEDIUM', true),
        ('11021111-0000-4000-8000-000000000051', 'Balance the following redox equation in acidic medium by ion-electron method:
MnO₄⁻ + Fe²⁺ -> Mn²⁺ + Fe³⁺', NULL, NULL, 'SA', 'Chemistry', 'Redox Reactions', 3, 'Reduction: MnO₄⁻ + 8H⁺ + 5e⁻ -> Mn²⁺ + 4H₂O. Oxidation: 5(Fe²⁺ -> Fe³⁺ + e⁻). Net: MnO₄⁻ + 5Fe²⁺ + 8H⁺ -> Mn²⁺ + 5Fe³⁺ + 4H₂O.', 'Two half-reactions (2M) + Balanced net equation (1M)', NULL, 'Reduction: MnO₄⁻ + 8H⁺ + 5e⁻ -> Mn²⁺ + 4H₂O. Oxidation: 5(Fe²⁺ -> Fe³⁺ + e⁻). Net: MnO₄⁻ + 5Fe²⁺ + 8H⁺ -> Mn²⁺ + 5Fe³⁺ + 4H₂O.', 'MEDIUM', true),
        ('11021111-0000-4000-8000-000000000052', 'Balance in basic medium:
MnO₄⁻ + I⁻ -> MnO₂ + I₂', NULL, NULL, 'SA', 'Chemistry', 'Redox Reactions', 3, '2(MnO₄⁻ + 2H₂O + 3e⁻ -> MnO₂ + 4OH⁻) and 3(2I⁻ -> I₂ + 2e⁻) => 2MnO₄⁻ + 6I⁻ + 4H₂O -> 2MnO₂ + 3I₂ + 8OH⁻.', 'Half-reactions (2M) + Balanced net (1M)', NULL, '2(MnO₄⁻ + 2H₂O + 3e⁻ -> MnO₂ + 4OH⁻) and 3(2I⁻ -> I₂ + 2e⁻) => 2MnO₄⁻ + 6I⁻ + 4H₂O -> 2MnO₂ + 3I₂ + 8OH⁻.', 'MEDIUM', true),
        ('11021111-0000-4000-8000-000000000053', 'Draw/describe a Galvanic Daniell cell setup. State the functions of the Salt Bridge, write anode and cathode half-cell reactions, cell representation, and calculate E°_cell if E°(Cu²⁺/Cu) = +0.34 V and E°(Zn²⁺/Zn) = -0.76 V.', NULL, NULL, 'LA', 'Chemistry', 'Redox Reactions', 5, 'Setup + Salt bridge completes internal circuit and maintains electrical neutrality (2M). Anode: Zn -> Zn²⁺ + 2e⁻; Cathode: Cu²⁺ + 2e⁻ -> Cu; Zn|Zn²⁺||Cu²⁺|Cu (1.5M). E°_cell = 0.34 - (-0.76) = +1.10 V (1.5M).', 'Cell & salt bridge (2M) + Half-reactions (1.5M) + E°=1.10V (1.5M)', NULL, 'Setup + Salt bridge completes internal circuit and maintains electrical neutrality (2M). Anode: Zn -> Zn²⁺ + 2e⁻; Cathode: Cu²⁺ + 2e⁻ -> Cu; Zn|Zn²⁺||Cu²⁺|Cu (1.5M). E°_cell = 0.34 - (-0.76) = +1.10 V (1.5M).', 'HARD', true),
        ('11021111-0000-4000-8000-000000000054', '(i) Arrange metals X(E°=-2.37 V), Y(E°=-0.76 V), Z(E°=+0.80 V) in decreasing order of reducing power. (ii) Can Z displace H₂ from dilute HCl? (iii) Identify oxidising and reducing agent in CuO + H₂ -> Cu + H₂O. (iv) Write Stock notation for SnCl₂ and MnO₂.', NULL, NULL, 'CASE', 'Chemistry', 'Redox Reactions', 4, '(i) X > Y > Z (more negative E° = stronger reducing agent). (ii) No (positive E° lies below H₂). (iii) Oxidising agent: CuO; Reducing agent: H₂. (iv) Sn(II)Cl₂ and Mn(IV)O₂.', '4 × 1M = 4M', 'Using the electrochemical series of standard electrode potentials, chemists predict spontaneous metal displacement and battery voltages without laboratory trial-and-error.', '(i) X > Y > Z (more negative E° = stronger reducing agent). (ii) No (positive E° lies below H₂). (iii) Oxidising agent: CuO; Reducing agent: H₂. (iv) Sn(II)Cl₂ and Mn(IV)O₂.', 'HARD', true),
        ('11021111-0000-4000-8000-000000000055', 'IUPAC name of CH₃-CH(OH)-CH₂-COOH is:', '{"A":"2-Hydroxybutanoic acid","B":"3-Hydroxybutanoic acid","C":"3-Carboxypropan-2-ol","D":"1-Hydroxybutanoic acid"}'::jsonb, 'B', 'MCQ', 'Chemistry', 'Organic Chemistry: Some Basic Principles and Techniques', 1, '-COOH gets priority number 1 -> -OH at C-3 -> 3-hydroxybutanoic acid.', NULL, NULL, '-COOH gets priority number 1 -> -OH at C-3 -> 3-hydroxybutanoic acid.', 'EASY', true),
        ('11021111-0000-4000-8000-000000000056', 'Stability order of carbocations among 1°, 2°, and 3° is:', '{"A":"1° > 2° > 3°","B":"3° > 2° > 1°","C":"2° > 3° > 1°","D":"All equal"}'::jsonb, 'B', 'MCQ', 'Chemistry', 'Organic Chemistry: Some Basic Principles and Techniques', 1, 'Three +I methyl groups and 9 hyperconjugative α-H stabilise 3° carbocation most.', NULL, NULL, 'Three +I methyl groups and 9 hyperconjugative α-H stabilise 3° carbocation most.', 'EASY', true),
        ('11021111-0000-4000-8000-000000000057', 'Permanent displacement of σ-electrons along a saturated carbon chain toward a more electronegative atom is:', '{"A":"Resonance effect","B":"Inductive effect (I-effect)","C":"Electromeric effect","D":"Hyperconjugation"}'::jsonb, 'B', 'MCQ', 'Chemistry', 'Organic Chemistry: Some Basic Principles and Techniques', 1, 'Inductive effect acts permanently via σ-bonds (fades after 3–4 carbons).', NULL, NULL, 'Inductive effect acts permanently via σ-bonds (fades after 3–4 carbons).', 'EASY', true),
        ('11021111-0000-4000-8000-000000000058', 'In Lassaigne''s test, Prussian blue colour confirms presence of:', '{"A":"Sulphur","B":"Nitrogen","C":"Chlorine","D":"Phosphorus"}'::jsonb, 'B', 'MCQ', 'Chemistry', 'Organic Chemistry: Some Basic Principles and Techniques', 1, 'Fe₄[Fe(CN)₆]₃ gives intense Prussian blue for Nitrogen.', NULL, NULL, 'Fe₄[Fe(CN)₆]₃ gives intense Prussian blue for Nitrogen.', 'EASY', true),
        ('11021111-0000-4000-8000-000000000059', 'Which pair illustrates Position Isomerism?', '{"A":"Propan-1-ol and Propan-2-ol","B":"Ethanol and Methoxymethane","C":"Butane and 2-Methylpropane","D":"Propanal and Propanone"}'::jsonb, 'A', 'MCQ', 'Chemistry', 'Organic Chemistry: Some Basic Principles and Techniques', 1, '-OH functional group shifts from C-1 to C-2 on same chain.', NULL, NULL, '-OH functional group shifts from C-1 to C-2 on same chain.', 'EASY', true),
        ('11021111-0000-4000-8000-00000000005a', 'Assertion (A): Electromeric effect is a temporary electron displacement observed only in multiple-bonded molecules.
Reason (R): Complete transfer of π-electrons occurs solely in the presence of an attacking reagent.
Choose the correct option:
A) Both A and R are true, and R is the correct explanation of A.
B) Both A and R are true, and R is not the correct explanation of A.
C) A is true but R is false.
D) A is false but R is true.', '{"A":"Both A and R are true, and R is the correct explanation of A","B":"Both A and R are true, and R is not the correct explanation of A","C":"A is true but R is false","D":"A is false but R is true"}'::jsonb, 'A', 'ASSERTION_REASON', 'Chemistry', 'Organic Chemistry: Some Basic Principles and Techniques', 1, 'Reversible temporary effect disappears when reagent leaves.', NULL, NULL, 'Reversible temporary effect disappears when reagent leaves.', 'EASY', true),
        ('11021111-0000-4000-8000-00000000005b', 'Define Homolytic vs Heterolytic bond cleavage and name the reactive intermediates formed in each.', NULL, NULL, 'VSA', 'Chemistry', 'Organic Chemistry: Some Basic Principles and Techniques', 2, 'Homolytic (symmetrical 1e⁻ each) forms neutral Free Radicals; Heterolytic (both e⁻ go to one atom) forms Carbocations and Carbanions.', '1M each', NULL, 'Homolytic (symmetrical 1e⁻ each) forms neutral Free Radicals; Heterolytic (both e⁻ go to one atom) forms Carbocations and Carbanions.', 'MEDIUM', true),
        ('11021111-0000-4000-8000-00000000005c', 'Write IUPAC names of (i) CH₃-CO-CH₂-CH₂-CH₃ and (ii) CH₂=CH-CH₂-Br.', NULL, NULL, 'VSA', 'Chemistry', 'Organic Chemistry: Some Basic Principles and Techniques', 2, '(i) Pentan-2-one; (ii) 3-Bromoprop-1-ene.', '1M each', NULL, '(i) Pentan-2-one; (ii) 3-Bromoprop-1-ene.', 'MEDIUM', true),
        ('11021111-0000-4000-8000-00000000005d', 'Distinguish between Electrophiles and Nucleophiles with two examples each (one neutral, one charged).', NULL, NULL, 'SA', 'Chemistry', 'Organic Chemistry: Some Basic Principles and Techniques', 3, 'Electrophiles (electron-loving, electron-deficient): NO₂⁺, BF₃ / AlCl₃. Nucleophiles (nucleus-loving, electron-rich): OH⁻ / CN⁻, NH₃ / H₂O.', '1.5M each', NULL, 'Electrophiles (electron-loving, electron-deficient): NO₂⁺, BF₃ / AlCl₃. Nucleophiles (nucleus-loving, electron-rich): OH⁻ / CN⁻, NH₃ / H₂O.', 'MEDIUM', true),
        ('11021111-0000-4000-8000-00000000005e', 'Explain Hyperconjugation ("no-bond resonance"). Why is (CH₃)₃C⁺ more stable than CH₃CH₂⁺?', NULL, NULL, 'SA', 'Chemistry', 'Organic Chemistry: Some Basic Principles and Techniques', 3, 'Delocalisation of C–H σ-electrons adjacent to a positively charged sp² carbon p-orbital. tert-Butyl carbocation has 9 α-hydrogens (9 hyperconjugative structures) vs only 3 α-hydrogens in ethyl carbocation.', 'Definition (1.5M) + Stability reason (1.5M)', NULL, 'Delocalisation of C–H σ-electrons adjacent to a positively charged sp² carbon p-orbital. tert-Butyl carbocation has 9 α-hydrogens (9 hyperconjugative structures) vs only 3 α-hydrogens in ethyl carbocation.', 'MEDIUM', true),
        ('11021111-0000-4000-8000-00000000005f', 'Explain the chemistry of Lassaigne''s Sodium Fusion Test for detecting (a) Nitrogen, (b) Sulphur (lead acetate + sodium nitroprusside), and (c) Halogens (AgNO₃ after boiling with conc. HNO₃). Why must Lassaigne extract be boiled with HNO₃ before testing halogens?', NULL, NULL, 'LA', 'Chemistry', 'Organic Chemistry: Some Basic Principles and Techniques', 5, 'Na + C + N -> NaCN -> Prussian blue Fe₄[Fe(CN)₆]₃ (2M). Na₂S gives black PbS or violet [Fe(CN)₅NOS]⁴⁻ (1.5M). NaX gives AgCl (white, NH₄OH soluble), AgBr (pale yellow), AgI (yellow). Boiling with HNO₃ decomposes interfering NaCN and Na₂S before adding AgNO₃ (1.5M).', 'N (2M) + S (1.5M) + X & HNO₃ reason (1.5M)', NULL, 'Na + C + N -> NaCN -> Prussian blue Fe₄[Fe(CN)₆]₃ (2M). Na₂S gives black PbS or violet [Fe(CN)₅NOS]⁴⁻ (1.5M). NaX gives AgCl (white, NH₄OH soluble), AgBr (pale yellow), AgI (yellow). Boiling with HNO₃ decomposes interfering NaCN and Na₂S before adding AgNO₃ (1.5M).', 'HARD', true),
        ('11021111-0000-4000-8000-000000000060', '(i) Technique used to separate aniline + water mixture. (ii) Method to separate glycerol from spent-lye in soap industry. (iii) Technique to separate two miscible liquids (chloroform b.p. 334 K & aniline b.p. 457 K). (iv) Principle behind paper/thin-layer chromatography.', NULL, NULL, 'CASE', 'Chemistry', 'Organic Chemistry: Some Basic Principles and Techniques', 4, '(i) Steam distillation. (ii) Distillation under reduced pressure. (iii) Simple distillation. (iv) Differential adsorption / partitioning of components between stationary phase and mobile phase.', '4 × 1M = 4M', 'Organic chemists purify crude synthesised liquids using tailored separation methods matching boiling points, thermal stability, and partition coefficients.', '(i) Steam distillation. (ii) Distillation under reduced pressure. (iii) Simple distillation. (iv) Differential adsorption / partitioning of components between stationary phase and mobile phase.', 'HARD', true),
        ('11021111-0000-4000-8000-000000000061', 'Alkanes react with Cl₂ in diffused sunlight by which mechanism?', '{"A":"Electrophilic addition","B":"Free-radical substitution","C":"Nucleophilic substitution","D":"Elimination"}'::jsonb, 'B', 'MCQ', 'Chemistry', 'Hydrocarbons', 1, 'Initiation, propagation, termination via free radicals.', NULL, NULL, 'Initiation, propagation, termination via free radicals.', 'EASY', true),
        ('11021111-0000-4000-8000-000000000062', 'Addition of HBr to propene yields 2-bromopropane as the major product according to:', '{"A":"Saytzeff rule","B":"Markovnikov''s rule","C":"Hund''s rule","D":"Kharasch peroxide effect"}'::jsonb, 'B', 'MCQ', 'Chemistry', 'Hydrocarbons', 1, 'Negative Br⁻ attaches to carbon with fewer hydrogens (via stable 2° carbocation).', NULL, NULL, 'Negative Br⁻ attaches to carbon with fewer hydrogens (via stable 2° carbocation).', 'EASY', true),
        ('11021111-0000-4000-8000-000000000063', 'In presence of organic peroxide, propene + HBr gives 1-bromopropane via:', '{"A":"Ionic mechanism","B":"Free-radical anti-Markovnikov Kharasch effect","C":"Elimination","D":"Friedel-Crafts"}'::jsonb, 'B', 'MCQ', 'Chemistry', 'Hydrocarbons', 1, 'Peroxide effect applies only to HBr, NOT HCl or HI.', NULL, NULL, 'Peroxide effect applies only to HBr, NOT HCl or HI.', 'EASY', true),
        ('11021111-0000-4000-8000-000000000064', 'Passing ethyne (acetylene) gas through a red-hot iron tube at 873 K produces:', '{"A":"Ethane","B":"Benzene (cyclic polymerisation)","C":"Hexane","D":"Naphthalene"}'::jsonb, 'B', 'MCQ', 'Chemistry', 'Hydrocarbons', 1, '3 CH≡CH -> C₆H₆ (aromatic benzene).', NULL, NULL, '3 CH≡CH -> C₆H₆ (aromatic benzene).', 'EASY', true),
        ('11021111-0000-4000-8000-000000000065', 'Which reagent converts alkyne R-C≡C-R selectively into cis-alkene vs trans-alkene?', '{"A":"Lindlar''s catalyst -> cis; Na/liquid NH₃ -> trans","B":"Both give cis","C":"KMnO₄","D":"HBr"}'::jsonb, 'A', 'MCQ', 'Chemistry', 'Hydrocarbons', 1, 'Pd/C-quinoline (Lindlar) gives cis; Birch reduction Na/liq NH₃ gives trans.', NULL, NULL, 'Pd/C-quinoline (Lindlar) gives cis; Birch reduction Na/liq NH₃ gives trans.', 'EASY', true),
        ('11021111-0000-4000-8000-000000000066', 'Assertion (A): Benzene undergoes electrophilic substitution reactions readily rather than electrophilic addition.
Reason (R): Addition would destroy benzene''s extra resonance stabilisation (~150 kJ/mol aromaticity), whereas substitution preserves the aromatic sextet.
Choose the correct option:
A) Both A and R are true, and R is the correct explanation of A.
B) Both A and R are true, and R is not the correct explanation of A.
C) A is true but R is false.
D) A is false but R is true.', '{"A":"Both A and R are true, and R is the correct explanation of A","B":"Both A and R are true, and R is not the correct explanation of A","C":"A is true but R is false","D":"A is false but R is true"}'::jsonb, 'A', 'ASSERTION_REASON', 'Chemistry', 'Hydrocarbons', 1, 'Resonance stability favours substitution.', NULL, NULL, 'Resonance stability favours substitution.', 'EASY', true),
        ('11021111-0000-4000-8000-000000000067', 'Write the Wurtz reaction with one example and state why it is unsuitable for preparing alkanes with an odd number of carbons.', NULL, NULL, 'VSA', 'Chemistry', 'Hydrocarbons', 2, '2 R-X + 2 Na --Dry Ether--> R-R + 2 NaX (e.g., bromoethane -> n-butane). Mixing two different haloalkanes yields a messy mixture of three alkanes hard to separate.', 'Equation (1M) + Odd-alkane limitation (1M)', NULL, '2 R-X + 2 Na --Dry Ether--> R-R + 2 NaX (e.g., bromoethane -> n-butane). Mixing two different haloalkanes yields a messy mixture of three alkanes hard to separate.', 'MEDIUM', true),
        ('11021111-0000-4000-8000-000000000068', 'Why is ethyne slightly acidic (forms sodium acetylide with Na metal), whereas ethene and ethane are not?', NULL, NULL, 'VSA', 'Chemistry', 'Hydrocarbons', 2, 'sp-hybridised carbons in ethyne have 50% s-character, making C strongly electronegative so it pulls C–H bond electrons tightly, easing H⁺ release.', '50% s-character electronegativity reason (2M)', NULL, 'sp-hybridised carbons in ethyne have 50% s-character, making C strongly electronegative so it pulls C–H bond electrons tightly, easing H⁺ release.', 'MEDIUM', true),
        ('11021111-0000-4000-8000-000000000069', 'Give balanced equations for Ozonolysis of (i) Propene and (ii) But-2-ene followed by Zn/H₂O hydrolysis.', NULL, NULL, 'SA', 'Chemistry', 'Hydrocarbons', 3, '(i) CH₃-CH=CH₂ -> CH₃CHO (ethanal) + HCHO (methanal). (ii) CH₃-CH=CH-CH₃ -> 2 CH₃CHO (ethanal).', '1.5M each', NULL, '(i) CH₃-CH=CH₂ -> CH₃CHO (ethanal) + HCHO (methanal). (ii) CH₃-CH=CH-CH₃ -> 2 CH₃CHO (ethanal).', 'MEDIUM', true),
        ('11021111-0000-4000-8000-00000000006a', 'State Huckel''s Rule of Aromaticity. Classify Cyclopentadienyl anion and Benzene as aromatic or non-aromatic.', NULL, NULL, 'SA', 'Chemistry', 'Hydrocarbons', 3, 'Planar, completely conjugated cyclic ring containing (4n + 2) π electrons (n=0,1,2...). Benzene (6π, n=1) is aromatic; Cyclopentadienyl anion (6π, n=1, planar conjugated) is aromatic.', 'Huckel rule (1.5M) + Classification (1.5M)', NULL, 'Planar, completely conjugated cyclic ring containing (4n + 2) π electrons (n=0,1,2...). Benzene (6π, n=1) is aromatic; Cyclopentadienyl anion (6π, n=1, planar conjugated) is aromatic.', 'MEDIUM', true),
        ('11021111-0000-4000-8000-00000000006b', 'Explain the Mechanism of Electrophilic Substitution in Benzene taking Nitration (HNO₃ + H₂SO₄ -> Nitrobenzene) or Friedel-Crafts Alkylation as example across all 3 steps: (i) Generation of electrophile, (ii) Formation of carbocation arenium intermediate, (iii) Loss of proton to restore aromaticity.', NULL, NULL, 'LA', 'Chemistry', 'Hydrocarbons', 5, 'Three clear mechanistic steps with curved arrow / resonance structures of arenium ion (4M) + Balanced reaction (1M).', '3 steps cleanly shown (4M) + Reaction (1M)', NULL, 'Three clear mechanistic steps with curved arrow / resonance structures of arenium ion (4M) + Balanced reaction (1M).', 'HARD', true),
        ('11021111-0000-4000-8000-00000000006c', '(i) Draw Newman projections of Staggered and Eclipsed conformations of ethane. (ii) Which conformation has lower energy and why? (iii) Identify ortho/para-directing vs meta-directing groups among -OH, -NO₂, -CH₃, -CHO. (iv) Reagent used to convert ethanol to ethene.', NULL, NULL, 'CASE', 'Chemistry', 'Hydrocarbons', 4, '(i) Staggered (60° dihedral) vs Eclipsed (0° dihedral). (ii) Staggered is ~12.5 kJ/mol more stable due to minimum torsional strain. (iii) o/p: -OH, -CH₃; meta: -NO₂, -CHO. (iv) Hot conc. H₂SO₄ at 443 K.', '4 × 1M = 4M', 'Conformational rotation about single C–C bonds and ring substituent directing effects dictate industrial hydrocarbon synthesis and regioselectivity.', '(i) Staggered (60° dihedral) vs Eclipsed (0° dihedral). (ii) Staggered is ~12.5 kJ/mol more stable due to minimum torsional strain. (iii) o/p: -OH, -CH₃; meta: -NO₂, -CHO. (iv) Hot conc. H₂SO₄ at 443 K.', 'HARD', true),
        ('11021111-0000-4000-8000-00000000006d', 'What volume of 2 M HCl solution is required to prepare 500 mL of 0.5 M HCl solution?', '{"A":"100 mL","B":"125 mL","C":"250 mL","D":"50 mL"}'::jsonb, 'B', 'MCQ', 'Chemistry', 'Some Basic Concepts', 1, 'Option (B) is correct. Using dilution law M₁V₁ = M₂V₂: 2 × V₁ = 0.5 × 500 => V₁ = 250/2 = 125 mL.', 'Apply M₁V₁ = M₂V₂ -> V₁ = 125 mL [1 Mark]', NULL, 'Examiner Tip: Volume units can stay in mL on both sides as long as they match. | Common Pitfall: Computing 250 mL without dividing by M₁ = 2.', 'EASY', true),
        ('11021111-0000-4000-8000-00000000006e', 'Which of the following sets of quantum numbers (n, l, m, s) is NOT permissible for an electron?', '{"A":"n = 3, l = 2, m = -2, s = +1/2","B":"n = 2, l = 1, m = 0, s = -1/2","C":"n = 3, l = 3, m = 0, s = +1/2","D":"n = 4, l = 0, m = 0, s = -1/2"}'::jsonb, 'C', 'MCQ', 'Chemistry', 'Structure of Atom', 1, 'Option (C) is correct. Azimuthal quantum number l can only take integer values from 0 to (n - 1). For n = 3, maximum l is 2, so l = 3 is invalid.', 'Identify l must satisfy 0 ≤ l ≤ n - 1 -> l = 3 invalid for n = 3 [1 Mark]', NULL, 'Examiner Tip: Check 3 rules: 0 ≤ l ≤ n-1, -l ≤ m ≤ +l, s = ±1/2. | Common Pitfall: Allowing l = n.', 'EASY', true),
        ('11021111-0000-4000-8000-00000000006f', 'The total number of radial (spherical) nodes present in a 4p orbital is:', '{"A":"1","B":"2","C":"3","D":"4"}'::jsonb, 'B', 'MCQ', 'Chemistry', 'Structure of Atom', 1, 'Option (B) is correct. Radial nodes = n - l - 1 = 4 - 1 - 1 = 2 (for 4p: n=4, l=1).', 'Radial nodes = n - l - 1 = 4 - 1 - 1 = 2 [1 Mark]', NULL, 'Examiner Tip: Radial nodes = n - l - 1; Angular nodes = l; Total nodes = n - 1. | Common Pitfall: Giving total nodes (n - 1 = 3) when radial nodes are asked.', 'EASY', true),
        ('11021111-0000-4000-8000-000000000070', 'Among B, C, N, O, the correct increasing order of first ionisation enthalpy (IE₁) is:', '{"A":"B < C < N < O","B":"B < C < O < N","C":"B < O < C < N","D":"C < B < O < N"}'::jsonb, 'B', 'MCQ', 'Chemistry', 'Periodicity', 1, 'Option (B) is correct. Generally IE₁ increases across Period 2, EXCEPT Nitrogen (2s² 2p³) has a stable half-filled p-subshell, making IE₁(N) > IE₁(O). Order: B < C < O < N.', 'Account for half-filled 2p³ stability of Nitrogen -> B < C < O < N [1 Mark]', NULL, 'Examiner Tip: Two classic Period 2 exceptions: IE₁(Be) > IE₁(B) and IE₁(N) > IE₁(O). | Common Pitfall: Following monotonic trend B < C < N < O.', 'EASY', true),
        ('11021111-0000-4000-8000-000000000071', 'According to VSEPR theory, the shape of ClF₃ molecule (with 3 bond pairs and 2 lone pairs on central Cl) is:', '{"A":"Trigonal planar","B":"Pyramidal","C":"Bent T-shaped","D":"Tetrahedral"}'::jsonb, 'C', 'MCQ', 'Chemistry', 'Chemical Bonding', 1, 'Option (C) is correct. Central Cl has sp³d hybridisation (5 electron pairs -> trigonal bipyramidal geometry) with 2 lone pairs at equatorial positions, giving a bent T-shaped molecular shape.', 'Identify 3 bp + 2 lp -> T-shaped geometry [1 Mark]', NULL, 'Examiner Tip: Lone pairs ALWAYS occupy equatorial positions in sp³d trigonal bipyramidal geometry to minimize lp-bp repulsions at 90°. | Common Pitfall: Placing lone pairs at axial positions giving trigonal planar.', 'EASY', true),
        ('11021111-0000-4000-8000-000000000072', 'Which of the following species is paramagnetic according to Molecular Orbital Theory (MOT)?', '{"A":"N₂","B":"O₂²⁻","C":"O₂","D":"F₂"}'::jsonb, 'C', 'MCQ', 'Chemistry', 'Chemical Bonding', 1, 'Option (C) is correct. O₂ has 16 electrons with two unpaired electrons in degenerate π*2px¹ π*2py¹ antibonding orbitals, making it paramagnetic (bond order = 2). N₂, O₂²⁻, and F₂ have all electrons paired.', 'Identify O₂ due to 2 unpaired electrons in π*2p orbitals [1 Mark]', NULL, 'Examiner Tip: Both O₂ (16 e⁻) and B₂ (10 e⁻) are famously paramagnetic in MOT. | Common Pitfall: Assuming O₂²⁻ (18 e⁻) is paramagnetic.', 'EASY', true),
        ('11021111-0000-4000-8000-000000000073', 'For a chemical process to be spontaneous at ALL temperatures, the signs of ΔH and ΔS must be respectively:', '{"A":"ΔH > 0, ΔS < 0","B":"ΔH < 0, ΔS > 0","C":"ΔH < 0, ΔS < 0","D":"ΔH > 0, ΔS > 0"}'::jsonb, 'B', 'MCQ', 'Chemistry', 'Thermodynamics', 1, 'Option (B) is correct. ΔG = ΔH - TΔS. When ΔH < 0 (exothermic) and ΔS > 0 (entropy increases), ΔG is negative at every positive temperature T.', 'ΔH < 0 and ΔS > 0 guarantees ΔG < 0 for all T [1 Mark]', NULL, 'Examiner Tip: Spontaneous criterion: ΔG < 0 at constant T & P. | Common Pitfall: Confusing signs of ΔH and ΔS.', 'EASY', true),
        ('11021111-0000-4000-8000-000000000074', 'The conjugate acid and conjugate base of HSO₄⁻ are respectively:', '{"A":"SO₄²⁻ and H₂SO₄","B":"H₂SO₄ and SO₄²⁻","C":"H₂SO₃ and SO₃²⁻","D":"H₃O⁺ and OH⁻"}'::jsonb, 'B', 'MCQ', 'Chemistry', 'Equilibrium', 1, 'Option (B) is correct. Adding H⁺ gives conjugate acid H₂SO₄; removing H⁺ gives conjugate base SO₄²⁻.', 'Gain H⁺ -> H₂SO₄ (acid); Lose H⁺ -> SO₄²⁻ (base) [1 Mark]', NULL, 'Examiner Tip: Conjugate Acid = species + H⁺; Conjugate Base = species - H⁺. | Common Pitfall: Reversing conjugate acid and conjugate base order.', 'EASY', true),
        ('11021111-0000-4000-8000-000000000075', 'In the reaction N₂(g) + 3H₂(g) ⇌ 2NH₃(g), ΔH = -92.4 kJ/mol, forward yield of ammonia is favoured by:', '{"A":"High temperature and low pressure","B":"Low temperature and high pressure","C":"Low temperature and low pressure","D":"Addition of inert gas at constant volume"}'::jsonb, 'B', 'MCQ', 'Chemistry', 'Equilibrium', 1, 'Option (B) is correct. The reaction is exothermic (ΔH < 0 -> favoured by low T) and proceeds with decrease in gaseous moles (4 -> 2 -> favoured by high P).', 'Exothermic -> low T; fewer product moles -> high P [1 Mark]', NULL, 'Examiner Tip: Always count Δn_g = moles_products - moles_reactants for gaseous pressure effect. | Common Pitfall: Choosing high temperature thinking it boosts rate (rate ≠ equilibrium yield).', 'EASY', true),
        ('11021111-0000-4000-8000-000000000076', 'The oxidation numbers of Cr in CrO₅ and S in H₂SO₅ (Caro''s acid) are respectively:', '{"A":"+10 and +8","B":"+6 and +6","C":"+6 and +8","D":"+5 and +6"}'::jsonb, 'B', 'MCQ', 'Chemistry', 'Redox Reactions', 1, 'Option (B) is correct. Both contain peroxide linkages (-O-O- where O is -1): CrO₅ has butterfly structure with 2 peroxide linkages (4 O at -1, 1 O at -2) => Cr = +6. H₂SO₅ has 1 peroxide linkage => S = +6.', 'Account for peroxide (-1) oxygen linkages -> +6 and +6 [1 Mark]', NULL, 'Examiner Tip: Maximum oxidation state of an element cannot exceed its group valence electrons (Cr = 6, S = 6)! | Common Pitfall: Naively assigning -2 to every oxygen and writing +10 or +8.', 'EASY', true),
        ('11021111-0000-4000-8000-000000000077', 'The IUPAC name of CH₃–CH(OH)–CH₂–COOH is:', '{"A":"2-Hydroxybutanoic acid","B":"3-Hydroxybutanoic acid","C":"3-Carboxybutan-2-ol","D":"1-Carboxypropan-2-ol"}'::jsonb, 'B', 'MCQ', 'Chemistry', 'Organic Chemistry', 1, 'Option (B) is correct. -COOH is the principal functional group (gets C-1): C1(COOH)-C2(CH₂)-C3(CH-OH)-C4(CH₃) -> 3-Hydroxybutanoic acid.', 'Number from -COOH -> -OH at C3 -> 3-Hydroxybutanoic acid [1 Mark]', NULL, 'Examiner Tip: Carboxylic acid (-COOH) always gets priority over -OH, becoming suffix "-oic acid" and prefix "hydroxy-". | Common Pitfall: Numbering from the CH₃ end.', 'EASY', true),
        ('11021111-0000-4000-8000-000000000078', 'Which of the following carbocations is the MOST stable due to hyperconjugation and inductive effect?', '{"A":"CH₃⁺","B":"CH₃CH₂⁺","C":"(CH₃)₂CH⁺","D":"(CH₃)₃C⁺"}'::jsonb, 'D', 'MCQ', 'Chemistry', 'Organic Chemistry', 1, 'Option (D) is correct. Tertiary butyl carbocation (CH₃)₃C⁺ has 9 α-hydrogens participating in hyperconjugation plus +I effect from 3 methyl groups, giving highest stability (3° > 2° > 1° > CH₃⁺).', 'Identify 3° carbocation (9 α-H hyperconjugation) [1 Mark]', NULL, 'Examiner Tip: Count α-hydrogens adjacent to C⁺: 9 > 6 > 3 > 0. | Common Pitfall: Choosing primary carbocation.', 'EASY', true),
        ('11021111-0000-4000-8000-000000000079', 'Assertion (A): Heisenberg''s uncertainty principle is insignificant in the motion of macroscopic objects like a cricket ball.
Reason (R): The value of Planck''s constant h (6.626 × 10⁻³⁴ J·s) is extremely small compared to macroscopic mass values.', '{"A":"Both A and R are true, and R is the correct explanation of A","B":"Both A and R are true, but R is not the correct explanation of A","C":"A is true, but R is false","D":"A is false, but R is true"}'::jsonb, 'A', 'ASSERTION_REASON', 'Chemistry', 'Atomic Structure', 1, 'Option (A) is correct. Δx·Δv ≥ h/(4πm). For macroscopic mass m ~ 0.1 kg, Δx·Δv ~ 10⁻³³ m²/s, which is negligible.', 'Option (A) with h/4πm scaling explanation [1 Mark]', NULL, 'Examiner Tip: Show numerical order ~10⁻³³ for macroscopic objects if asked in 2M. | Common Pitfall: Saying macroscopic balls have zero uncertainty theoretically (it is non-zero, just negligible).', 'EASY', true),
        ('11021111-0000-4000-8000-00000000007a', 'Assertion (A): Chlorine has a more negative electron gain enthalpy than Fluorine.
Reason (R): Fluorine has a very small compact 2p-orbital where incoming electrons suffer strong inter-electronic repulsion.', '{"A":"Both A and R are true, and R is the correct explanation of A","B":"Both A and R are true, but R is not the correct explanation of A","C":"A is true, but R is false","D":"A is false, but R is true"}'::jsonb, 'A', 'ASSERTION_REASON', 'Chemistry', 'Periodicity', 1, 'Option (A) is correct. Adding an electron to compact n=2 shell of F causes strong electron-electron repulsion compared to larger n=3 shell of Cl.', 'Option (A) citing compact 2p inter-electronic repulsion in F [1 Mark]', NULL, 'Examiner Tip: Same reasoning applies to Oxygen vs Sulphur (Δ_eg H of S is more negative than O). | Common Pitfall: Thinking F has higher negative electron gain enthalpy because it is more electronegative.', 'EASY', true),
        ('11021111-0000-4000-8000-00000000007b', 'Assertion (A): The dipole moment of NH₃ (1.47 D) is greater than that of NF₃ (0.23 D).
Reason (R): In NH₃, the orbital dipole of the lone pair points in the same direction as the resultant N–H bond dipoles, whereas in NF₃ it opposes the resultant N–F bond dipoles.', '{"A":"Both A and R are true, and R is the correct explanation of A","B":"Both A and R are true, but R is not the correct explanation of A","C":"A is true, but R is false","D":"A is false, but R is true"}'::jsonb, 'A', 'ASSERTION_REASON', 'Chemistry', 'Chemical Bonding', 1, 'Option (A) is correct. Both molecules are pyramidal, but in NH₃ bond dipoles point towards N (reinforcing lone pair dipole), while in NF₃ electronegative F pulls dipoles away from N (opposing lone pair dipole).', 'Option (A) contrasting vector directions of N-H vs N-F dipoles [1 Mark]', NULL, 'Examiner Tip: Draw small dipole arrows on pyramidal NH₃ and NF₃ for 2M/3M questions. | Common Pitfall: Assuming higher electronegativity of F automatically gives NF₃ a larger dipole moment.', 'EASY', true),
        ('11021111-0000-4000-8000-00000000007c', 'Assertion (A): Addition of CH₃COONa to an aqueous solution of CH₃COOH suppresses the ionisation of acetic acid.
Reason (R): CH₃COONa is a strong electrolyte supplying common CH₃COO⁻ ions that shift the equilibrium backward by Le Chatelier''s principle.', '{"A":"Both A and R are true, and R is the correct explanation of A","B":"Both A and R are true, but R is not the correct explanation of A","C":"A is true, but R is false","D":"A is false, but R is true"}'::jsonb, 'A', 'ASSERTION_REASON', 'Chemistry', 'Equilibrium', 1, 'Option (A) is correct. Common ion effect suppresses dissociation of weak acid CH₃COOH when common acetate ions increase.', 'Option (A) identifying Common Ion Effect [1 Mark]', NULL, 'Examiner Tip: This mixture (CH₃COOH + CH₃COONa) also forms an Acidic Buffer! | Common Pitfall: Thinking pH decreases instead of increasing when CH₃COONa is added.', 'EASY', true),
        ('11021111-0000-4000-8000-00000000007d', '50.0 kg of N₂(g) and 10.0 kg of H₂(g) are mixed to produce NH₃(g). Identify the limiting reagent and calculate the mass of NH₃(g) formed.', NULL, NULL, 'VSA', 'Chemistry', 'Some Basic Concepts', 2, 'N₂ + 3H₂ → 2NH₃.
Moles of N₂ = 50,000 / 28 = 1785.7 mol.
Moles of H₂ = 10,000 / 2.0 = 5000 mol.
1785.7 mol N₂ requires 3 × 1785.7 = 5357.1 mol H₂, which exceeds available 5000 mol H₂. Thus H₂ is the limiting reagent.
From 3 mol H₂ -> 2 mol NH₃ => 5000 mol H₂ produces (2/3) × 5000 = 3333.3 mol NH₃.
Mass of NH₃ = 3333.3 × 17 g ≈ 56,667 g = 56.67 kg.', 'Identify H₂ as limiting reagent using mole ratio [1 Mark] | Calculate 56.67 kg NH₃ formed [1 Mark]', NULL, 'Examiner Tip: Convert kg to grams cleanly when dividing by molar mass in g/mol. | Common Pitfall: Assuming N₂ is limiting without comparing 1:3 stoichiometric ratio.', 'MEDIUM', true),
        ('11021111-0000-4000-8000-00000000007e', 'Calculate the de Broglie wavelength of a ball of mass 0.1 kg moving with a velocity of 10 m/s (h = 6.626 × 10⁻³⁴ J·s).', NULL, NULL, 'VSA', 'Chemistry', 'Structure of Atom', 2, 'λ = h / (mv) = (6.626 × 10⁻³⁴) / (0.1 × 10) = 6.626 × 10⁻³⁴ m.', 'State λ = h/(mv) [1 Mark] | Compute 6.626 × 10⁻³⁴ m [1 Mark]', NULL, 'Examiner Tip: Add 1-line note why wave nature is unobservable at 10⁻³⁴ m scale. | Common Pitfall: Calculation slip with 0.1 × 10 = 1.', 'MEDIUM', true),
        ('11021111-0000-4000-8000-00000000007f', 'If water vapour is assumed to be a perfect gas, molar enthalpy change for vaporisation of 1 mol of water at 1 bar and 100°C is 41 kJ/mol. Calculate the internal energy change ΔU when 1 mol of water is vaporised at 1 bar and 100°C (R = 8.314 J/mol·K).', NULL, NULL, 'VSA', 'Chemistry', 'Thermodynamics', 2, 'H₂O(l) → H₂O(g). Δn_g = 1 - 0 = 1 mol.
Using ΔH = ΔU + Δn_g R T => ΔU = ΔH - Δn_g R T.
T = 100 + 273 = 373 K.
Δn_g R T = 1 × 8.314 × 373 = 3101.1 J = 3.10 kJ.
ΔU = 41 - 3.10 = 37.90 kJ/mol.', 'Identify Δn_g = 1 & formula ΔU = ΔH - Δn_gRT [1 Mark] | Convert J to kJ -> ΔU = 37.90 kJ/mol [1 Mark]', NULL, 'Examiner Tip: Never forget to convert R·T from Joules to kilojoules before subtracting from kJ! | Common Pitfall: Subtracting 3101 directly from 41 without unit matching.', 'MEDIUM', true),
        ('11021111-0000-4000-8000-000000000080', 'What is a buffer solution? Calculate the pH of a 0.001 M aqueous NaOH solution at 298 K (Kw = 10⁻¹⁴).', NULL, NULL, 'VSA', 'Chemistry', 'Equilibrium', 2, 'Buffer solution: A solution that resists change in pH upon dilution or addition of small amounts of acid or alkali.
For 0.001 M NaOH: [OH⁻] = 10⁻³ M.
pOH = -log₁₀(10⁻³) = 3.
pH = 14 - pOH = 14 - 3 = 11.', 'Definition of buffer solution [1 Mark] | Compute pOH = 3 -> pH = 11 [1 Mark]', NULL, 'Examiner Tip: Always double check that a base gives pH > 7! | Common Pitfall: Reporting pOH = 3 as pH = 3 for NaOH.', 'MEDIUM', true),
        ('11021111-0000-4000-8000-000000000081', 'Give two differences between Inductive effect (I-effect) and Resonance / Mesomeric effect (R/M-effect).', NULL, NULL, 'VSA', 'Chemistry', 'Organic Chemistry', 2, '1. Inductive effect involves partial displacement of sigma (σ) electrons along a saturated chain and weakens rapidly after 3 carbons; Resonance effect involves complete delocalisation of pi (π) or lone-pair electrons through conjugated systems.
2. Resonance effect operates uniformly across the entire conjugated system without fading significantly with distance.', 'σ vs π/lone pair electron involvement [1 Mark] | Distance decay vs full conjugation propagation [1 Mark]', NULL, 'Examiner Tip: Mention "permanent effects" for both I and R effects. | Common Pitfall: Confusing permanent Resonance effect with temporary Electromeric effect.', 'MEDIUM', true),
        ('11021111-0000-4000-8000-000000000082', 'An organic compound contains 4.07% hydrogen, 24.27% carbon and 71.65% chlorine by mass. Its molar mass is 98.96 g/mol. Determine its empirical and molecular formulas. (Atomic masses: C = 12, H = 1, Cl = 35.5)', NULL, NULL, 'SA', 'Chemistry', 'Some Basic Concepts', 3, 'Moles per 100 g:
• C: 24.27 / 12 = 2.02 mol
• H: 4.07 / 1 = 4.07 mol
• Cl: 71.65 / 35.5 = 2.02 mol
Simplest molar ratio (divide by 2.02): C : H : Cl = 1 : 2 : 1.
Empirical formula = CH₂Cl.
Empirical formula mass = 12 + 2 + 35.5 = 49.5 g/mol.
n = Molar mass / Empirical mass = 98.96 / 49.5 ≈ 2.
Molecular formula = (CH₂Cl)₂ = C₂H₄Cl₂.', 'Mole calculation & dividing by smallest mole (2.02) [1.5 Marks] | Empirical formula CH₂Cl & mass 49.5 [1 Mark] | n = 2 -> Molecular formula C₂H₄Cl₂ [0.5 Mark]', NULL, 'Examiner Tip: NCERT textbook solved example — show the tabular mole/ratio calculation cleanly. | Common Pitfall: Rounding 4.07/2.02 = 2.01 incorrectly.', 'MEDIUM', true),
        ('11021111-0000-4000-8000-000000000083', 'Calculate the wavelength and frequency of light emitted when an electron in a hydrogen atom transitions from n = 4 to n = 2 energy level (R_H = 1.097 × 10⁷ m⁻¹, c = 3 × 10⁸ m/s). Name the spectral series.', NULL, NULL, 'SA', 'Chemistry', 'Structure of Atom', 3, '1/λ = R_H (1/n₁² - 1/n₂²) = 1.097 × 10⁷ (1/2² - 1/4²) = 1.097 × 10⁷ × (3/16)
= 2.057 × 10⁶ m⁻¹.
λ = 1 / (2.057 × 10⁶) ≈ 4.86 × 10⁻⁷ m = 486 nm (blue-green visible light).
Frequency ν = c / λ = 3 × 10⁸ / (4.86 × 10⁻⁷) ≈ 6.17 × 10¹⁴ Hz.
Spectral series: Balmer series (since n₁ = 2).', 'Rydberg formula 1/λ with n₁=2, n₂=4 [1 Mark] | λ = 486 nm & ν = 6.17 × 10¹⁴ Hz [1.5 Marks] | Identify Balmer series [0.5 Mark]', NULL, 'Examiner Tip: Balmer series (n₁ = 2) lies in the visible region! | Common Pitfall: Forgetting to take reciprocal of 1/λ.', 'MEDIUM', true),
        ('11021111-0000-4000-8000-000000000084', 'Using valence bond hybridisation theory, explain the hybridisation state, geometry, and bond angles of:
(a) CH₄
(b) PCl₅
(c) SF₆.', NULL, NULL, 'SA', 'Chemistry', 'Chemical Bonding', 3, '(a) CH₄: sp³ hybridisation of C -> Tetrahedral geometry, all bond angles = 109.5°.
(b) PCl₅: sp³d hybridisation of P -> Trigonal bipyramidal geometry; 3 equatorial bonds at 120° and 2 longer axial bonds at 90° to equatorial plane.
(c) SF₆: sp³d² hybridisation of S -> Regular Octahedral geometry, all bond angles = 90°.', '(a) CH₄ sp³, tetrahedral, 109.5° [1 Mark] | (b) PCl₅ sp³d, TBP, 120° & 90° (longer axial) [1 Mark] | (c) SF₆ sp³d², octahedral, 90° [1 Mark]', NULL, 'Examiner Tip: Note why axial bonds in PCl₅ are longer/weaker: 3 bond-pair repulsions at 90° vs 2 for equatorial. | Common Pitfall: Writing all angles in PCl₅ as 109.5° or 90°.', 'MEDIUM', true),
        ('11021111-0000-4000-8000-000000000085', 'State Hess''s Law of Constant Heat Summation. Calculate the standard enthalpy of formation of CH₄(g) from:
(i) C(s) + O₂(g) → CO₂(g), ΔH° = -393.5 kJ/mol
(ii) H₂(g) + ½O₂(g) → H₂O(l), ΔH° = -285.8 kJ/mol
(iii) CH₄(g) + 2O₂(g) → CO₂(g) + 2H₂O(l), ΔH° = -890.3 kJ/mol', NULL, NULL, 'SA', 'Chemistry', 'Thermodynamics', 3, 'Hess''s Law: Total enthalpy change of a reaction is identical whether carried out in one step or multiple steps.
Target: C(s) + 2H₂(g) → CH₄(g).
Operate: Eq(i) + 2×Eq(ii) - Eq(iii):
Δ_f H°(CH₄) = (-393.5) + 2(-285.8) - (-890.3)
= -393.5 - 571.6 + 890.3 = -965.1 + 890.3 = -74.8 kJ/mol.', 'Statement of Hess''s Law [1 Mark] | Target eq + algebraic combination (i) + 2(ii) - (iii) [1 Mark] | Result Δ_f H° = -74.8 kJ/mol [1 Mark]', NULL, 'Examiner Tip: Double Eq(ii) because 2 moles of H₂ are needed to form CH₄. | Common Pitfall: Forgetting to multiply -285.8 by 2 or flipping sign of Eq(iii).', 'MEDIUM', true),
        ('11021111-0000-4000-8000-000000000086', 'Balance the following redox reaction in acidic medium by half-reaction (ion-electron) method:
Cr₂O₇²⁻(aq) + Fe²⁺(aq) → Cr³⁺(aq) + Fe³⁺(aq)', NULL, NULL, 'SA', 'Chemistry', 'Redox Reactions', 3, 'Reduction half: Cr₂O₇²⁻ + 14H⁺ + 6e⁻ → 2Cr³⁺ + 7H₂O
Oxidation half: Fe²⁺ → Fe³⁺ + e⁻ (multiply by 6 -> 6Fe²⁺ → 6Fe³⁺ + 6e⁻)
Adding both halves to cancel 6e⁻:
Cr₂O₇²⁻(aq) + 6Fe²⁺(aq) + 14H⁺(aq) → 2Cr³⁺(aq) + 6Fe³⁺(aq) + 7H₂O(l).', 'Balanced reduction half with 14H⁺ & 6e⁻ [1.5 Marks] | Multiply oxidation half by 6 & add [1.5 Marks]', NULL, 'Examiner Tip: Verify BOTH atoms AND net charge on LHS (-2 + 12 + 14 = +24) and RHS (6 + 18 = +24)! | Common Pitfall: Forgetting to multiply Cr³⁺ by 2 when balancing Cr atoms.', 'MEDIUM', true),
        ('11021111-0000-4000-8000-000000000087', 'Draw all possible structural isomers of Pentane (C₅H₁₂) and give their IUPAC names. State why chain isomerism arises here.', NULL, NULL, 'SA', 'Chemistry', 'Organic Chemistry', 3, 'Chain isomerism arises when compounds share the same molecular formula but differ in the carbon skeleton.
1. CH₃–CH₂–CH₂–CH₂–CH₃ -> Pentane (n-pentane)
2. CH₃–CH(CH₃)–CH₂–CH₃ -> 2-Methylbutane (isopentane)
3. C(CH₃)₄ -> 2,2-Dimethylpropane (neopentane).', '3 correct structural formulas [1.5 Marks] | Exact IUPAC names of all 3 isomers [1.5 Marks]', NULL, 'Examiner Tip: Note C(CH₃)₄ IUPAC name is 2,2-dimethylpropane (longest chain is 3 carbons). | Common Pitfall: Drawing 2-ethylpropane (actually identical to 2-methylbutane!).', 'MEDIUM', true),
        ('11021111-0000-4000-8000-000000000088', 'Define solubility product (K_sp). Calculate the molar solubility of AgCl in water at 298 K if its K_sp = 1.6 × 10⁻¹⁰.', NULL, NULL, 'SA', 'Chemistry', 'Equilibrium', 3, 'K_sp is the product of equilibrium molar concentrations of constituent ions in a saturated solution of a sparingly soluble salt, each raised to its stoichiometric coefficient.
AgCl(s) ⇌ Ag⁺(aq) + Cl⁻(aq). Let solubility = s mol/L.
K_sp = [Ag⁺][Cl⁻] = s × s = s² = 1.6 × 10⁻¹⁰ = 16 × 10⁻¹¹? Better: 1.6 × 10⁻¹⁰ = 160 × 10⁻¹² => s = √(1.6 × 10⁻¹⁰) ≈ 1.265 × 10⁻⁵ mol/L.', 'Definition of K_sp [1 Mark] | Set K_sp = s² and solve s = 1.265 × 10⁻⁵ M [2 Marks]', NULL, 'Examiner Tip: Adjust even power of 10 before taking square root: √(1.6 × 10⁻¹⁰) = √1.6 × 10⁻⁵. | Common Pitfall: Writing K_sp = 4s² instead of s² for 1:1 AB salt.', 'MEDIUM', true),
        ('11021111-0000-4000-8000-000000000089', 'Read the passage and answer:
Elements show systematic periodicity in atomic radii, ionisation enthalpy, and electronegativity across periods and down groups. Across a period left to right, effective nuclear charge increases while valence shell remains fixed.

(a) Why are cations smaller and anions larger than their parent neutral atoms? (1M)
(b) Arrange the isoelectronic ions N³⁻, O²⁻, F⁻, Na⁺, Mg²⁺ in increasing order of ionic radii. (1M)
(c) Why is the second ionisation enthalpy (IE₂) of Sodium far higher than that of Magnesium? (2M)', NULL, NULL, 'CASE', 'Chemistry', 'Case Study', 4, '(a) Cations have higher Z_eff per electron (often losing a shell), shrinking radius; anions have extra electron repulsion and lower Z_eff per electron, expanding radius.
(b) Increasing ionic radius (all have 10 e⁻): Mg²⁺ < Na⁺ < F⁻ < O²⁻ < N³⁻.
(c) After losing 1 e⁻, Na⁺ attains a stable noble-gas octet (2s² 2p⁶), requiring massive energy for IE₂, whereas Mg⁺ (3s¹) loses its 2nd electron easily to reach the noble-gas configuration.', '(a) Z_eff / repulsion explanation [1 Mark] | (b) Mg²⁺ < Na⁺ < F⁻ < O²⁻ < N³⁻ [1 Mark] | (c) Noble-gas core of Na⁺ vs 3s¹ of Mg⁺ [2 Marks]', NULL, 'Examiner Tip: For isoelectronic species, higher positive nuclear charge Z -> smaller radius. | Common Pitfall: Reversing ionic radius order of isoelectronic ions.', 'HARD', true),
        ('11021111-0000-4000-8000-00000000008a', 'Read the passage and answer:
Molecular Orbital Theory predicts bond order, magnetic character, and relative stability of diatomic species. Up to N₂ (14 electrons), σ2pz lies ABOVE π2px, π2py due to 2s-2p mixing; from O₂ onwards, σ2pz lies BELOW π2px, π2py.

(a) Write the formula for Bond Order in MOT. (1M)
(b) Compute the bond orders of O₂, O₂⁺, and O₂⁻. (1.5M)
(c) Arrange O₂, O₂⁺, and O₂⁻ in increasing order of bond length and bond stability. (1.5M)', NULL, NULL, 'CASE', 'Chemistry', 'Case Study', 4, '(a) Bond Order = ½ (N_b - N_a), where N_b = bonding e⁻, N_a = antibonding e⁻.
(b) O₂: BO = 2.0; O₂⁺ (one e⁻ removed from π*): BO = 2.5; O₂⁻ (one e⁻ added to π*): BO = 1.5.
(c) Higher bond order => shorter bond & higher stability:
Stability order: O₂⁻ < O₂ < O₂⁺; Bond length order: O₂⁺ < O₂ < O₂⁻.', '(a) Formula ½(Nb - Na) [1 Mark] | (b) BOs 2.0, 2.5, 1.5 [1.5 Marks] | (c) Stability & length orders [1.5 Marks]', NULL, 'Examiner Tip: Remember: Stability ∝ Bond Order, while Bond Length ∝ 1 / Bond Order! | Common Pitfall: Making bond length order identical to stability order.', 'HARD', true),
        ('11021111-0000-4000-8000-00000000008b', '(a) State (i) Aufbau Principle, (ii) Pauli Exclusion Principle, and (iii) Hund''s Rule of Maximum Multiplicity.
(b) Explain why Chromium (Z = 24) and Copper (Z = 29) have anomalous ground-state electronic configurations ([Ar]3d⁵4s¹ and [Ar]3d¹⁰4s¹).
(c) List all four quantum numbers for the valence 3d electron of Scandium (Z = 21).', NULL, NULL, 'LA', 'Chemistry', 'Structure of Atom', 5, '(a) (i) Aufbau: Orbitals are filled in increasing order of energy (n + l rule).
(ii) Pauli: No two electrons in an atom can have the exact same set of all four quantum numbers (max 2 e⁻ per orbital with opposite spins).
(iii) Hund: Pairing of electrons in degenerate orbitals does not occur until each orbital is singly occupied with parallel spins.
(b) Half-filled (d⁵) and fully-filled (d¹⁰) subshells acquire extra stability due to symmetrical electron distribution and maximum exchange energy.
(c) 3d¹ in Sc: n = 3, l = 2, m = -2 (any from -2 to +2), s = +1/2.', '(a) All 3 electronic rules stated accurately [2 Marks] | (b) Symmetrical distribution + exchange energy for Cr & Cu [2 Marks] | (c) Quantum numbers for 3d¹ [1 Mark]', NULL, 'Examiner Tip: Include BOTH "symmetry" and "exchange energy" for Cr and Cu stability. | Common Pitfall: Writing 3d⁴ 4s² for Cr.', 'HARD', true),
        ('11021111-0000-4000-8000-00000000008c', '(a) Calculate the pH of a 0.02 M acetic acid (CH₃COOH) solution if its ionisation constant K_a = 1.8 × 10⁻⁵ (given √3.6 ≈ 1.9).
(b) State Le Chatelier''s Principle and predict the effect of (i) adding inert gas at constant pressure, and (ii) increasing temperature on PCl₅(g) ⇌ PCl₃(g) + Cl₂(g), ΔH = +92.5 kJ/mol.', NULL, NULL, 'LA', 'Chemistry', 'Chemical Equilibrium', 5, '(a) [H⁺] = √(K_a × C) = √(1.8 × 10⁻⁵ × 0.02) = √(3.6 × 10⁻⁷) = √(0.36 × 10⁻⁶) = 0.6 × 10⁻³ = 6 × 10⁻⁴ M.
pH = -log₁₀(6 × 10⁻⁴) = 4 - log 6 = 4 - 0.778 = 3.22.
(b) Le Chatelier: Any change in T, P or concentration shifts equilibrium to counteract the imposed stress.
(i) Inert gas at constant P increases total volume -> shifts toward MORE moles (forward, 1 -> 2 moles).
(ii) Endothermic ΔH > 0 -> increasing T shifts equilibrium FORWARD.', '(a) [H⁺] = √(Ka·C) = 6×10⁻⁴ M -> pH = 3.22 [2.5 Marks] | (b) Le Chatelier + both predictions justified [2.5 Marks]', NULL, 'Examiner Tip: Distinguish inert gas at constant VOLUME (no shift) vs constant PRESSURE (shifts toward more moles)! | Common Pitfall: Saying inert gas at constant pressure has no effect.', 'HARD', true),
        ('11021111-0000-4000-8000-00000000008d', '(a) Explain why benzene undergoes electrophilic substitution rather than addition reactions despite having three double bonds.
(b) Write balanced chemical equations for:
  (i) Nitration of benzene
  (ii) Friedel-Crafts alkylation with CH₃Cl
  (iii) Ozonolysis of But-2-ene followed by Zn/H₂O.
(c) Arrange Ethane, Ethene, and Ethyne in increasing order of C–C bond length.', NULL, NULL, 'LA', 'Chemistry', 'Hydrocarbons', 5, '(a) Benzene has 6 delocalised π-electrons forming an exceptionally stable aromatic sextet (resonance energy ~150 kJ/mol). Addition destroys aromaticity, whereas electrophilic substitution preserves the aromatic ring.
(b) (i) C₆H₆ + conc. HNO₃ --(conc. H₂SO₄, 323-333K)--> C₆H₅NO₂ + H₂O.
(ii) C₆H₆ + CH₃Cl --(Anhyd. AlCl₃)--> C₆H₅CH₃ (Toluene) + HCl.
(iii) CH₃–CH=CH–CH₃ + O₃ --(Zn/H₂O)--> 2 CH₃CHO (Ethanal).
(c) Bond length order: Ethyne (120 pm) < Ethene (134 pm) < Ethane (154 pm).', '(a) Aromaticity & resonance stability preservation [1.5 Marks] | (b) All 3 organic reactions with reagents/conditions [2.5 Marks] | (c) C≡C < C=C < C-C bond length order [1 Mark]', NULL, 'Examiner Tip: Always write "Anhydrous AlCl₃" for Friedel-Crafts and "conc. H₂SO₄" for nitration. | Common Pitfall: Missing Zn/H₂O reductive cleavage in ozonolysis.', 'HARD', true),
        ('11021111-0000-4000-8000-00000000008e', 'Reaction of propene (CH₃–CH=CH₂) with HBr in the presence of benzoyl peroxide yields:', '{"A":"2-Bromopropane","B":"1-Bromopropane","C":"1,2-Dibromopropane","D":"Propyne"}'::jsonb, 'B', 'MCQ', 'Chemistry', 'Hydrocarbons', 1, 'Option (B) is correct. In presence of organic peroxide (Kharasch / anti-Markovnikov free-radical mechanism), HBr adds to propene giving 1-bromopropane as the major product.', 'Anti-Markovnikov peroxide effect (HBr only) -> 1-bromopropane [1 Mark]', NULL, 'Examiner Tip: Remember: Peroxide effect works ONLY with HBr, NOT with HCl or HI! | Common Pitfall: Applying Markovnikov rule and selecting 2-bromopropane.', 'EASY', true),
        ('11021111-0000-4000-8000-00000000008f', 'Which of the following hydrocarbons reacts with ammoniacal AgNO₃ (Tollens'' reagent) to form a white precipitate?', '{"A":"But-2-yne","B":"But-1-yne","C":"Ethene","D":"Ethane"}'::jsonb, 'B', 'MCQ', 'Chemistry', 'Hydrocarbons', 1, 'Option (B) is correct. Terminal alkynes (like But-1-yne, CH₃CH₂C≡CH) possess an acidic sp-hybridized C–H hydrogen that reacts with Tollens'' reagent.', 'Identify terminal alkyne But-1-yne with acidic ≡C-H [1 Mark]', NULL, 'Examiner Tip: Internal alkynes (like But-2-yne) have no acidic terminal hydrogen and do not react. | Common Pitfall: Selecting But-2-yne.', 'EASY', true),
        ('11021111-0000-4000-8000-000000000090', 'Write the chemical equation for Wurtz reaction using bromoethane. Why is Wurtz reaction NOT suitable for preparing alkanes with an odd number of carbon atoms?', NULL, NULL, 'VSA', 'Chemistry', 'Hydrocarbons', 2, 'Equation: 2 CH₃CH₂Br + 2Na --(Dry Ether)--> CH₃–CH₂–CH₂–CH₃ (n-Butane) + 2NaBr.
Limitation: Preparing an odd-carbon alkane requires two different alkyl halides (R-X and R''-X), producing a mixture of three alkanes (R-R, R''-R'', and R-R'') with close boiling points that are difficult to separate.', 'Balanced Wurtz equation in Dry Ether -> Butane [1 Mark] | Mixture of 3 alkanes problem explained [1 Mark]', NULL, 'Examiner Tip: Always specify "Dry Ether" over arrow in Wurtz reaction. | Common Pitfall: Forgetting Na reacts violently with moisture if dry ether is absent.', 'MEDIUM', true)
